      "full_cot": "The compound interest formula isn't: }.\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Understand the Problem:**\n   - Dr. Fu's bank account has a nominal annual interest rate of 6%. This rate is compounded monthly.\n   - We need to find the equivalent annual interest rate (r) that would provide the same return if compounded annually.\n\n2. **Recall Compound Interest Formula:**\n   - The compound interest formula is:\n     \\[\n     A = P \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n\n     \\]\n     where:\n     - \\( A \\) is the amount of money accumulated after n years, including interest.\n     - \\( P \\) is the principal amount (the initial amount of money).\n     - \\( r \\) is the annual interest rate (decimal).\n     - \\( n \\) is the number of times that interest is compounded per year.\n\n3. **Determine the Effective Annual Rate (EAR):**\n   - When interest is compounded more frequently, the EAR is higher. The formula for EAR when compounded monthly is:\n     \\[\n     \\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + \\frac{r_{\\text{nominal}}}{n}\\right)^n - 1\n     \\]\n     where \\( r_{\\text{nominal}} \\) is the nominal annual interest rate, and \\( n \\) is the number of compounding periods per year.\n\n4. **Plug in the Known Values:**\n   - \\( r_{\\text{nominal}} = 6\\% = 0.06 \\) (convert percentage to decimal)\n   - Compounded monthly means \\( n = 12 \\).\n\n5. **Calculate the EAR:**\n   - Substitute into the EAR formula:\n     \\[\n     \\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} - 1\n     \\]\n   - Calculate \\( \\frac{0.06}{12} \\):\n     \\[\n     \\frac{0.06}{12} = 0.005\n     \\]\n   - So, it becomes:\n     \\[\n     \\text{EAR} = (1 + 0.005)^{12} - 1\n     \\]\n   - Calculate \\( (1.005)^{12} \\). I can use a calculator for this. Let me compute that:\n     \\[\n     1.005^{12} \\approx 1.064727\n     \\]\n   - Subtract 1 from that result:\n     \\[\n     \\text{EAR} \\approx 1.064727 - 1 = 0.064727\n     \\]\n   - Convert this back to a percentage:\n     \\[\n     0.064727 \\times 100\\% \\approx 6.4727\\%\n     \\]\n   - Rounded to the nearest hundredth, that's approximately 6.47%.\n\n6. **Conclusion:**\n   - The equivalent annual interest rate that would yield the same return as the monthly compounded 6% rate is approximately 6.47%.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.47}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) that would provide the same return as a bank account compounded monthly at an annual interest rate of 6%, we need to calculate the effective annual rate (EAR).\n\n1. **Recall the formula for the effective annual rate (EAR) when compounded monthly:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + \\frac{r_{\\text{nominal}}}{n}\\right)^n - 1\n   \\]\n   where \\( r_{\\text{nominal}} \\) is the nominal annual interest rate and \\( n \\) is the number of compounding periods per year.\n\n2. **Plug in the known values:**\n   - \\( r_{\\text{nominal}} = 0.06 \\) (6% as a decimal)\n   - \\( n = 12 \\) (compounded monthly)\n\n3. **Calculate the EAR:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} - 1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{0.06}{12} = 0.005\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{EAR} = (1 + 0.005)^{12} - 1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   1.005^{12} \\approx 1.064727\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{EAR} \\approx 1.064727 - 1 = 0.064727\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   0.064727 \\times 100\\% \\approx 6.4727\\%\n   \\]\n\n4. **Round to the nearest hundredth:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{EAR} \\approx 6.47\\%\n   \\]\n\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.47}.",
