6167780961775 - 1 = 0.06167780961775, which is approximately 6.167780961775%, which rounds to 6.17% when rounded to the nearest hundredth.\n\nWait, so in this case, my step-by-step manual calculation now gives me 6.17% when I do it accurately. But earlier, I had a miscalculation where I thought it was 6.18%. So, which is correct? It seems that the accurate value is approximately 6.1678%, so 6.17% when rounded.\n\nBut wait, using a calculator, I can compute 1.005^12 accurately. Let me try that.\n\nUsing a calculator, 1.005^12. So, 1.005 multiplied by itself 12 times.\n\nLet me use logarithms for more precision. Let me compute ln(1.005) first.\n\nln(1.005) ≈ 0.004979.\n\nMultiply by 12: 0.004979 * 12 ≈ 0.059748.\n\nNow, compute e^0.059748. Using a calculator, e^0.059748 ≈ 1.06167.\n\nTherefore, (1.005)^12 ≈ 1.06167, so the effective annual rate is 6.167%, which is 6.17% when rounded.\n\nTherefore, the correct value is 6.17%.\n\nWait, but in my manual step-by-step, I got 1.06167780961775, which is approximately 1.061678, so 6.1678%, which is 6.17% when rounded.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 6.17%.\n\nWait, but earlier, I had a slightly different result. So, perhaps the discrepancy comes from the number of decimal places I carry during each multiplication. When I did the first step-by-step, I was truncating the decimals, which led to a slightly higher value. But when I do precise multiplication, I get 1.06167780961775, which is approximately 6.1678%, so 6.17%.\n\nTherefore, the equivalent annual rate is 6.17%.\n\nWait, but earlier, I thought I had 6.18% because of a miscalculation. So, to confirm, let's compute 1.005^12 using precise exponentiation. Alternatively, since I know that (1 + 0.06/12)^12 = (1.005)^12, which is approximately 1.06167, so 6.167%, which is 6.17% when rounded.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 6.17%.\n\nWait, but another thought: maybe using the formula for the effective annual rate, which is (1 + (r/n))^n - 1. So, (1 + 0.06/12)^12 - 1. So, that is exactly 1.06167 - 1 = 0.06167, which is 6.167%, so 6.17%.\n\nTherefore, the equivalent annual rate is 6.17%.\n\nSo, in conclusion, after double-checking, I can confidently say that the equivalent annual rate is 6.17%.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{6.17}\n</think>\n\nTo find the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) that would give the same return if compounded annually instead of monthly, we need to calculate the effective annual rate (EAR). \n\nThe given annual interest rate is 6%, compounded monthly. The formula for the effective annual rate is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n - 1\n\\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( r \\) is the annual nominal interest rate (6% or 0.06).\n- \\( n \\) is the number of compounding periods per year (12 for monthly).\n\nSubstituting the values:\n\n\\[\n\\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} - 1\n\\]\n\nFirst, compute \\( \\frac{0.06}{12} \\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{0.06}{12} = 0.005\n\\]\n\nThen, calculate \\( (1 + 0.005)^{12} \\):\n\n\\[\n(1.005)^{12} \\approx 1.06167\n\\]\n\nSubtract 1 to find the EAR:\n\n\\[\n1.06167 - 1 = 0.06167\n\\]\n\nConvert this to a percentage:\n\n\\[\n0.06167 \\times 100 \\approx 6.167\\%\n\\]\n\nRounding to the nearest hundredth, we get:\n\n\\[\nr \\approx 6.17\\%\n\\]\n\nThus, the equivalent annual interest rate is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6.17}\n\\]",
