2\n\nSo, actually, it's approximately 1.0616778. So, subtracting 1, the EAR is approximately 0.0616778 or 6.16778%. So, rounding to the nearest hundredth, 6.17%.\n\nWait, but in my first step-by-step calculation, I thought it came out to 1.061061, but that was a miscalculation. So, the correct value is approximately 1.0616778, leading to 6.17%.\n\nAlternatively, using the formula:\n\nEAR = (1 + 0.06/12)^12 - 1 = (1.005)^12 - 1 ≈ 1.0616778 - 1 = 0.0616778 ≈ 6.16778%, so 6.17% when rounded to the nearest hundredth.\n\nTherefore, the equivalent annual interest rate r is 6.17%.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.17}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) percent for Dr. Fu Manchu's bank account that compounds monthly at an annual rate of 6 percent, we need to calculate the effective annual rate (EAR).\n\nThe formula for the effective annual rate is:\n\\[ \\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n - 1 \\]\nwhere:\n- \\( r \\) is the nominal annual interest rate (6% or 0.06 in decimal).\n- \\( n \\) is the number of times interest is compounded per year (12 for monthly).\n\nSubstituting the values:\n\\[ \\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} - 1 \\]\n\\[ \\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + 0.005\\right)^{12} - 1 \\]\n\\[ \\text{EAR} = (1.005)^{12} - 1 \\]\n\nCalculating \\( (1.005)^{12} \\):\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} \\approx 1.0616778 \\]\n\nThus, the effective annual rate is:\n\\[ \\text{EAR} \\approx 1.0616778 - 1 = 0.0616778 \\]\nConverting this to a percentage:\n\\[ 0.0616778 \\approx 6.16778\\% \\]\n\nRounding to the nearest hundredth:\n\\[ r \\approx 6.17\\% \\]\n\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.17}.",
