      "full_cot": "- \\( A_{monthly} \\) as the amount after one month with monthly compounding. }.\n\nWait, I need to write this step by step. Let me start fresh.\n\nGiven:\n- Annual interest rate (nominal) = 6% = 0.06 in decimal.\n- Compounded monthly, so n = 12.\n\nWe need to find the equivalent annual compounding rate r, such that the amount after one year is the same whether compounded monthly at 6% or compounded annually at r%.\n\nSo, using the compound interest formula for both cases.\n\nFirst, compute the amount when compounded monthly:\n\\[ A_{monthly} = P \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} \\]\n\nSecond, compute the amount when compounded annually:\n\\[ A_{annual} = P \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{1}\\right)^1 = P(1 + r) \\]\n\nSince both amounts are equal after one year:\n\\[ P \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = P(1 + r) \\]\n\nWe can divide both sides by P to cancel it out:\n\\[ \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\nTherefore, to find r, we can compute:\n\\[ r = \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} - 1 \\]\n\nNow, let's compute this step by step.\n\nFirst, calculate \\(\\frac{0.06}{12}\\):\n\\[ \\frac{0.06}{12} = 0.005 \\]\n\nThen, add 1 to it:\n\\[ 1 + 0.005 = 1.005 \\]\n\nNow, raise this to the 12th power:\nI can compute this using logarithms or a calculator, but since I don't have a calculator handy, maybe I can approximate it or recall that (1 + 0.005)^12 is approximately e^(0.005*12) = e^0.06 ≈ 1.0618365. But wait, that's an approximation using the continuous compounding formula, which might not be accurate for this case. Maybe better to compute it step by step or recall that the exact value is needed.\n\nAlternatively, I can use the formula for effective annual rate directly, which is:\n\\[ \\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n - 1 \\]\nHere, \\( r = 0.06 \\), \\( n = 12 \\). So, plug these in:\n\\[ \\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} - 1 \\]\n\\[ \\text{EAR} = (1.005)^{12} - 1 \\]\n\nCalculat