      "full_cot": "- \\( A_{annual} \\) as the amount after one year with monthly compounding. } I think I need to use the formula for effective annual rate. The effective annual rate is calculated by taking the monthly compounding rate and compounding it for one year, which is similar to calculating the amount after one year with monthly compounding and then finding the equivalent annual rate that would produce the same amount. The formula for effective annual rate is: \\[ \\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + \\frac{r_{monthly}}{n}\\right)^n - 1 \\] Where \\( r_{monthly} \\) is the monthly interest rate. In this case, the nominal rate is 6%, which is the annual rate. So, the monthly rate \\( r_{monthly} \\) would be \\( \\frac{6\\%}{12} \\) or 0.06 divided by 12. Let me compute that. 0.06 divided by 12 is 0.005. So, the monthly rate is 0.005 or 0.5%. Now, to find the effective annual rate, I need to compute \\( (1 + 0.005)^{12} - 1 \\). Let me compute \\( (1.005)^{12} \\). Hmm, I don't remember the exact value, but I can approximate it. Alternatively, I can use the formula for compound interest where the amount is \\( P \\times (1 + \\frac{r}{n})^n \\). Since we are looking for the equivalent annual rate, the amount after one year should be the same whether we compound monthly or annually. So, if I take the amount after one year with monthly compounding, it would be \\( P \\times (1 + 0.005)^{12} \\). If it were compounded annually, the amount after one year would be \\( P \\times (1 + r_{annual}) \\). Therefore, setting these equal: \\[ P \\times (1 + 0.005)^{12} = P \\times (1 + r_{annual}) \\] Since \\( P \\) is the same on both sides, I can divide both sides by \\( P \\) to get: \\[ (1 + 0.005)^{12} = 1 + r_{annual} \\] So, solving for \\( r_{annual} \\), subtract 1 from both sides: \\[ (1 + 0.005)^{12} - 1 = r_{annual} \\] Therefore, \\( r_{annual} \\) is the effective annual rate. Let me compute \\( (1.005)^{12} \\). I can use a calculator for this. 1.005 multiplied by i