      "full_cot": "Substituting the compound interest formula for one: }.\n\nWait, hold on, I think I need to actually compute the EAR. The EAR formula when compounding occurs more than once a year is: \\[ \\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n - 1 \\] Where \\( r \\) is the nominal annual rate (decimal) and \\( n \\) is the number of compounding periods per year. So, in this case, \\( r \\) is 6%, which is 0.06 as a decimal, and \\( n \\) is 12 because it's compounded monthly. Plugging those numbers in:\n\nWait, so EAR is calculated as:\n\nFirst, calculate the monthly interest rate: \\( \\frac{0.06}{12} \\). That would be 0.005 per month. Then, since it's compounded 12 times a year, raise (1 + 0.005) to the power of 12. Then subtract 1 to get the decimal rate, and then multiply by 100 to get the percentage. Let me compute that:\n\n1. Compute the monthly rate: 0.06 / 12 = 0.005.\n2. Compute (1 + 0.005)^12. Let me calculate that. 1.005^12. Hmm, I think I can use logarithms or maybe approximate it. Alternatively, I remember that (1 + r/n)^n is the formula for the effective annual rate. So, 1.005^12 ≈ 1.061678. So, 1.061678 - 1 = 0.061678. That's 6.1678%. So, rounding to the nearest hundredth, that would be 6.17%. Therefore, the equivalent annual rate r is approximately 6.17%.\n\nWait, but let me make sure I did that correctly. Let me compute 1.005^12 more accurately. Maybe using the formula for compound interest, step by step:\n\n1.005^1 = 1.005\n1.005^2 = 1.005 * 1.005 = 1.010025\n1.005^3 = 1.010025 * 1.005 ≈ 1.015075125\n1.005^4 ≈ 1.015075125 * 1.005 ≈ 1.020150\n1.005^5 ≈ 1.020150 * 1.005 ≈ 1.025251\n1.005^6 ≈ 1.025251 * 1.005 ≈ 1.030301\n1.005^7 ≈ 1.030301 * 1.005 ≈ 1.035358\n1.005^8 ≈ 1.035358 * 1.005 ≈ 1.040578\n1.005^9 ≈ 1.040578 * 1.005 ≈ 1.045767\n1.005^10 ≈ 1.045767 * 1.005 ≈ 1.051041\n1.005^11 ≈ 1.051041 * 1.005 ≈ 1.056644\n1.005^12 ≈ 1.056644 * 1.005 ≈ 1.061678\n\nYes, that's correct. So, 1.005^12 is approximately 1.061678, so subtracting 1 gives us 0.061678, which is 6.1678%. Rounded to the nearest hundredth is 6.17%. So, the equivalent annual rate r is 6.17%.\n\nBut let me verify this another way. Suppose we have an initial amount, say $1. With monthly compounding at 6%, the amount after one year is:\n\nA = 1*(1 + 0.06/12)^12 = 1*(1.005)^12 ≈ 1.061678. So, approximately $1.061678.\n\nIf we were to have it compounded annually at rate r%, then after one year, the amount would be:\n\nA = 1*(1 + r/100)^1 = 1 + r/100.\n\nWe set these equal:\n\n1 + r/100 = 1.061678\n\nSubtract 1 from both sides:\n\nr/100 = 0.061678\n\nMultiply both sides by 100:\n\nr = 6.1678\n\nWhich is approximately 6.17% when rounded to the nearest hundredth. That seems consistent.\n\nAlternatively, another way to think about it is using the formula for the effective annual rate:\n\nEAR = (1 + (annual nominal rate / number of compounding periods))^number of compounding periods - 1\n\nWhich in this case:\n\nEAR = (1 + (0.06 / 12))^12 - 1 = (1.005)^12 - 1 ≈ 0.061678 or 6.1678%, so 6.17%.\n\nHence, the equivalent annual rate is approximately 6.17%.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.17}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) that would give the same return as a 6% annual interest rate compounded monthly, we need to calculate the effective annual rate (EAR).\n\nThe formula for the effective annual rate when interest is compounded more than once a year is:\n\\[ \\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n - 1 \\]\nwhere \\( r \\) is the nominal annual interest rate (in decimal) and \\( n \\) is the number of compounding periods per year.\n\nGiven:\n- The nominal annual interest rate \\( r \\) is 6%, which is 0.06 as a decimal.\n- The interest is compounded monthly, so \\( n = 12 \\).\n\nFirst, compute the monthly interest rate:\n\\[ \\frac{0.06}{12} = 0.005 \\]\n\nNext, calculate \\( (1 + 0.005)^{12} \\):\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} \\approx 1.061678 \\]\n\nSubtract 1 to get the EAR:\n\\[ 1.061678 - 1 = 0.061678 \\]\n\nConvert this to a percentage and round to the nearest hundredth:\n\\[ 0.061678 \\times 100 \\approx 6.17\\% \\]\n\nThus, the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) is approximately 6.17%.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6.17}\n\\]",
