p, I got 1.061364, leading to 6.1364%, which was 6.14%.\n\nBut when I computed using the logarithm approximation, I got 1.0616767, which would be 6.16767%, which is 6.17%.\n\nAnd when I did the step-by-step multiplication up to 12th power, I got approximately 1.0616778, so 6.16778%, which is 6.17%.\n\nSo, the discrepancy arises because when I did the step-by-step up to the 12th power, I got a slightly higher value.\n\nHmm, so which one is more accurate? Since I computed it manually up to 12 steps, perhaps that's more precise than the logarithm approximation. So, 6.17% is more accurate than 6.14%.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I made an error in the manual multiplication. Let me check some of the intermediate steps.\n\nWait, when I computed 1.005^12 as 1.0616778, that's accurate. So, that's the precise value.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can use a calculator for precise computation, but since I don't have one, I can try another approach.\n\nAlternatively, using the formula:\n\n(1 + r/n)^n = e^(r)\n\nWait, that's an approximation for large n, but n=12 is not that large, so perhaps it's not very accurate. Let me see:\n\nr = 0.06, n=12.\n\nSo, e^0.06 ≈ 1.06183654.\n\nWait, but 1.06183654 is higher than the actual (1.005)^12, which was approximately 1.0616778.\n\nSo, e^r is an overestimate here.\n\nSo, perhaps 6.17% is more accurate.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can use the formula for EAR:\n\nEAR = (1 + r/n)^n - 1.\n\nSo, if (1 + 0.06/12)^12 ≈ 1.0616778, then EAR ≈ 6.16778%, which is 6.17% when rounded to the nearest hundredth.\n\nTherefore, the equivalent annual rate is approximately 6.17%.\n\nWait, but earlier I thought my manual multiplication gave me 1.061364, which is 6.1364%, which is 6.14%.\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the manual multiplication earlier.\n\nWait, let me go back to the manual multiplication:\n\n1.005^1 = 1.005\n\n1.005^2 = 1.010025\n\n1.005^3 = 1.015075125\n\n1.005^4 = 1.020150500625\n\n1.005^5 = 1.0252512534375\n\n1.005^6 = 1.03037751015625\n\n1