1. After one year, with monthly compounding, the amount is:\n\n\\( A = 1 \\times (1 + 0.06/12)^{12} \\approx 1.061364 \\)\n\nSo, the amount is approximately $1.061364. If this were compounded annually at a rate of r%, then:\n\n\\( A = 1 \\times (1 + r) \\)\n\nSetting them equal:\n\n\\( 1.061364 = 1 + r \\implies r = 0.061364 \\implies 6.1364\\% \\)\n\nWhich is about 6.14% when rounded to the nearest hundredth. So, that seems consistent. Therefore, the equivalent annual interest rate is approximately 6.14%.\n\nAlternatively, I can use the formula for EAR:\n\n\\( \\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n - 1 \\)\n\nPlugging in r = 0.06 and n = 12:\n\n\\( \\text{EAR} = (1 + 0.06/12)^{12} - 1 \\)\n\nAs above, which is 1.061364 - 1 = 0.061364, which is 6.1364%, so 6.14% as before. So, that's the equivalent annual rate. So, the value of r is approximately 6.14%.\n\nWait, but just to make sure, is this correct? Because 6% compounded monthly is more than 6% compounded annually, which makes sense since the interest is compounded more frequently, so the rate should be a bit higher. 6.14% is indeed higher than 6%, so that seems reasonable.\n\nAlternatively, another way to compute this is using the formula:\n\n\\( \\text{EAR} = e^{r \\times (1 + \\frac{r}{n}) / n} - 1 \\)\n\nBut actually, I think the formula I used earlier is correct.\n\nWait, no, that's the continuous compounding formula. For continuous compounding, the formula is:\n\n\\( A = P e^{rt} \\)\n\nBut in this case, it's monthly compounding, not continuous. So, the correct formula is indeed \\( (1 + \\frac{r}{n})^n \\). So, I think my calculation is correct.\n\nAlternatively, let me use logarithms to compute 1.005^12 more accurately.\n\nTaking natural log of 1.005: ln(1.005) ≈ 0.004975\n\nThen, ln(1.005^12) = 12 * 0.004975 ≈ 0.0597\n\nExponentiating that: e^0.0597 ≈ 1.0613\n\nSo, that's consistent with my earlier manual calculation. So, 1.0613, so the EAR is approximately 6.13%, which rounds to 6.13% or 6.14% d