eturn as a 6 percent annual interest rate compounded monthly, we need to calculate the effective annual rate (EAR).\n\nThe formula for the effective annual rate is:\n\\[ \\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n - 1 \\]\nwhere \\( r \\) is the nominal annual interest rate and \\( n \\) is the number of times interest is compounded per year.\n\nGiven:\n- \\( r = 6\\% = 0.06 \\)\n- \\( n = 12 \\) (monthly compounding)\n\nFirst, we calculate \\( \\frac{r}{n} \\):\n\\[ \\frac{0.06}{12} = 0.005 \\]\n\nNext, we compute \\( (1 + 0.005)^{12} \\):\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} \\approx 1.0616778 \\]\n\nSubtracting 1 from this result gives the EAR:\n\\[ \\text{EAR} = 1.0616778 - 1 = 0.0616778 \\]\n\nConverting this to a percentage and rounding to the nearest hundredth:\n\\[ \\text{EAR} \\approx 6.17\\% \\]\n\nThus, the equivalent annual interest rate is:\n\\[ \\boxed{6.17\\%} \\]",
