      "full_cot": "First, compute the inside of the right side: r= 7.275\\%}\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Understand the Problem:**\n   - We have a bank account with an annual nominal interest rate of 6%, compounded monthly.\n   - We need to find the equivalent annual interest rate (r) that would produce the same return if compounded annually.\n\n2. **Recall the Compound Interest Formula:**\n   - The formula for compound interest is:\n     \\[ A = P \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n \\]\n   - Where:\n     - \\( A \\) = amount of money accumulated after n years, including interest.\n     - \\( P \\) = principal amount.\n     - \\( r \\) = annual interest rate (in decimal).\n     - \\( n \\) = number of times that interest is compounded per year.\n\n3. **Identify the Given Values:**\n   - The annual nominal interest rate (r nominal) = 6% = 0.06 in decimal.\n   - Compounded monthly, so \\( n = 12 \\).\n   - We need to find the equivalent annual interest rate (r annual), which will be compounded once per year.\n\n4. **Set Up the Equation for Equivalent Rates:**\n   - Since both compounding methods are for one year, the amounts should be equal.\n     - Amount after 1 year with monthly compounding:\n       \\[ A_{\\text{monthly}} = P \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} \\]\n     - Amount after 1 year with annual compounding:\n       \\[ A_{\\text{annual}} = P \\left(1 + r \\right) \\]\n   - Therefore, set \\( A_{\\text{monthly}} = A_{\\text{annual}} \\):\n     \\[ P \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = P \\left(1 + r \\right) \\]\n   - The principal \\( P \\) cancels out:\n     \\[ \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\n5. **Compute the Left Side of the Equation:**\n   - Calculate \\( \\frac{0.06}{12} \\):\n     \\[ \\frac{0.06}{12} = 0.005 \\]\n   - So, the equation becomes:\n     \\[ (1 + 0.005)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n   - Calculate \\( (1.005)^{12} \\):\n     - This is the effective annual rate (EAR) factor.\n     - Using a calculator:\n       \\[ 1.005^{12} \\approx 1.0648766 \\]\n\n6. **Solve for r:**\n   - Subtract 1 from both sides:\n     \\[ 1.0648766 - 1 = 0.0648766 \\]\n   - So, \\( r = 0.0648766 \\) as a decimal.\n   - Convert decimal to a percentage:\n     \\[ r \\approx 6.48766\\% \\]\n\n7. **Round to the Nearest Hundredth:**\n   - The value of \\( r \\) is approximately 6.48766%, which is 6.49% when rounded to the nearest hundredth.\n\n8. **Final Answer:**\n   - The equivalent annual interest rate is approximately 6.49%.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.49\\%}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) that would produce the same return as a 6% annual interest rate compounded monthly, we use the concept of the effective annual rate (EAR). \n\n1. The compound interest formula for monthly compounding is:\n   \\[\n   A = P \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n\n   \\]\n   where \\( r = 0.06 \\) and \\( n = 12 \\).\n\n2. For one year, the amount after monthly compounding is:\n   \\[\n   A_{\\text{monthly}} = P \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12}\n   \\]\n\n3. For one year, the amount after annual compounding is:\n   \\[\n   A_{\\text{annual}} = P \\left(1 + r \\right)\n   \\]\n\n4. Setting these amounts equal:\n   \\[\n   P \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = P \\left(1 + r \\right)\n   \\]\n\n5. The principal \\( P \\) cancels out:\n   \\[\n   \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = 1 + r\n   \\]\n\n6. Calculate the left side:\n   \\[\n   \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = \\left(1 + 0.005\\right)^{12} \\approx 1.0648766\n   \\]\n\n7. Solve for \\( r \\):\n   \\[\n   1.0648766 = 1 + r \\implies r \\approx 0.0648766\n   \\]\n\n8. Convert to a percentage and round to the nearest hundredth:\n   \\[\n   r \\approx 6.49\\%\n   \\]\n\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.49\\%}.",
