mpute 1.056395828 * 1.005:\n\n1 * 1.005 = 1.005, 0.056395828 * 1.005 ≈ approx 0.056395828 + 0.056395828 * 0.005 ≈ 0.056395828 + 0.000281979 ≈ 0.056677807. So total ≈ 1.005 + 0.056677807 ≈ 1.061677807.\n\nSo, after computing each month step by step, the amount after 12 months is approximately 1.061677807. So, that's the factor for monthly compounding. Therefore, to find the equivalent annual rate, we have:\n\n1 + r = 1.061677807\n\nSo, subtracting 1 from both sides:\n\nr = 0.061677807\n\nSo, converting that to a percentage, r is approximately 6.1677807%. The question asks for the answer to the nearest hundredth, so that would be 6.17%.\n\nWait, but let me verify my step-by-step computation because it took a lot of steps, and I might have made a mistake somewhere. Alternatively, maybe I can compute (1.005)^12 using logarithms.\n\nUsing logarithms:\n\nTake natural log of both sides:\n\nln(1.061677807) ≈ ?\n\nI know that ln(1.06) is approximately 0.058268908. Let's compute ln(1.061677807). I can use the Taylor series approximation for ln(1+x) around x=0: ln(1+x) ≈ x - x^2/2 + x^3/3 - x^4/4 + ... But maybe it's better to recall that ln(1.06) ≈ 0.0582689, ln(1.061677807) is a bit higher. Alternatively, since I don't have a calculator, maybe I can estimate it.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps use the formula for compound interest. The exact value of (1.005)^12 can be found using the formula, but since I don't have a calculator, my step-by-step calculation gave me approximately 1.0616778, which would make r approximately 6.16778%, so 6.17% when rounded to the nearest hundredth.\n\nAlternatively, I can recall that the formula for the effective annual rate is (1 + r/n)^n - 1, where r is the nominal rate and n is the number of compounding periods per year. So here, r is 6%, n is 12. So, (1 + 0.06/12)^12 - 1 ≈ 6.1677%.\n\nYes, that seems consistent.\n\nSo, to recap:\n\nEffective Annual Rate (EAR) = (1 + (nominal rate / number of compounding periods))^number of compounding periods - 1\n\nSo, EAR = (1 + 0.06/12)^12 - 1 ≈ 1.0616778 - 1 = 0.0616778, which is 6.16778%, so approximately 6.17%.\n\nTherefore, the equivalent annual rate is 6.17%.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.17}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) that would give the same return as a 6% annual interest rate compounded monthly, we need to calculate the effective annual rate (EAR).\n\nThe compound interest formula for one year is:\n\\[ A = P \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n \\]\n\nFor monthly compounding, the nominal rate is 6%, or 0.06 in decimal, and it is compounded 12 times a year. We set the amount after one year for both monthly and annual compounding equal:\n\\[ P \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = P (1 + r) \\]\n\nThe principal \\( P \\) cancels out, so we have:\n\\[ \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\nCalculating the inside of the left side:\n\\[ 1 + \\frac{0.06}{12} = 1 + 0.005 = 1.005 \\]\n\nNow, we need to compute \\( (1.005)^{12} \\):\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} \\approx 1.0616778 \\]\n\nThus, the effective annual rate \\( r \\) is:\n\\[ r = 1.0616778 - 1 = 0.0616778 \\]\n\nConverting this to a percentage and rounding to the nearest hundredth:\n\\[ r \\approx 6.17\\% \\]\n\nTherefore, the equivalent annual interest rate is \\(\\boxed{6.17}\\).",
