al rate.\n\nBut wait, just to make sure I didn't make a calculation error earlier. Let me compute 1.005^12 using another method. Maybe use the fact that ln(1.005) is approximately 0.004975, so ln(1.005^12) = 12 * 0.004975 = 0.0597, so exponentiating, e^0.0597 ≈ 1.0618, which is about 1.0618, so same as before.\n\nTherefore, the effective annual rate is approximately 6.18%, which rounds to 6.19%. Hmm, but I feel like maybe I remember that 6% compounded monthly is roughly 6.1678%, which would round to 6.17%, but I might have miscalculated.\n\nWait, perhaps my manual calculation was a bit off. Let me compute 1.005^12 more accurately.\n\nLet's try this:\n\n1.005^1 = 1.005\n\n1.005^2 = 1.005 * 1.005 = 1.010025\n\n1.005^3 = 1.010025 * 1.005 = 1.010025 + 1.010025 * 0.005 = 1.010025 + 0.005050125 = 1.015075125\n\n1.005^4 = 1.015075125 * 1.005 = 1.015075125 + 1.015075125 * 0.005 ≈ 1.015075125 + 0.0050753756 ≈ 1.0201505006\n\n1.005^5 ≈ 1.0201505006 * 1.005 ≈ 1.0201505006 + 1.0201505006 * 0.005 ≈ 1.0201505006 + 0.005100752 ≈ 1.0252512526\n\n1.005^6 ≈ 1.0252512526 * 1.005 ≈ 1.0252512526 + 1.0252512526 * 0.005 ≈ 1.0252512526 + 0.0051262563 ≈ 1.0303775089\n\n1.005^7 ≈ 1.0303775089 * 1.005 ≈ 1.0303775089 + 1.0303775089 * 0.005 ≈ 1.0303775089 + 0.005151887 ≈ 1.0355293959\n\n1.005^8 ≈ 1.0355293959 * 1.005 ≈ 1.0355293959 + 1.0355293959 * 0.005 ≈ 1.0355293959 + 0.005177647 ≈ 1.0407070429\n\n1.005^9 ≈ 1.0407070429 * 1.005 ≈ 1.0407070429 + 1.0407070429 * 0.005 ≈ 1.0407070429 + 0.005203535 ≈ 1.045910578\n\n1.005^10 ≈ 1.045910578 * 1.005 ≈ 1.045910578 + 1.045910578 * 0.005 ≈ 1.045910578 + 0.0052295529 ≈ 1.0511401309\n\n1.005^11 ≈ 1.0511401309 * 1.005 ≈ 1.0511401309 + 1.0511401309 * 0.005 ≈ 1.0511401309 + 0.0052557007 ≈ 1.0563958316\n\n1.005^12 ≈ 1.0563958316 * 1.005 ≈ 1.0563958316 + 1.0563958316 * 0.005 ≈ 1.0563958316 + 0.005281979 ≈ 1.0616778106\n\nSo, actually, it's approximately 1.0616778, which is about 6.16778%. So, that would round to 6.17%, not 6.19%.\n\nHmm, so my initial manual calculation was a bit off, but the accurate computation shows that it's approximately 6.1678%, which is 6.17% when rounded to the nearest hundredth.\n\nTherefore, I think the correct answer is 6.17%.\n\nWait, but earlier when I thought of it as 1.061855, that was because of a miscalculation step. So, actually, it's about 6.1678%, which is closer to 6.17% than 6.19%.\n\nSo, perhaps 6.17% is the accurate figure.\n\nAlternatively, let me use the formula for the effective annual rate, which is \\( (1 + \\frac{r}{n})^n \\), so:\n\n\\( (1 + 0.06/12)^{12} = (1.005)^{12} ≈ 1.0616778 \\), so 1.0616778 - 1 = 0.0616778, which is 6.16778%, so 6.17%.\n\nYes, so 6.17% is correct.\n\nWait, but why did I get 1.061855 earlier? Maybe I added incorrectly. Let me compute 1.005^12 using a more precise method.\n\nAlternatively, I can use the formula for compound interest:\n\n\\( A = P (1 + \\frac{0.06}{12})^{12} \\)\n\nSo, \\( A = P (1 + 0.005)^{12} \\approx P * 1.0616778 \\)\n\nSo, that's 6.16778% effective annual rate, so 6.17% when rounded to the nearest hundredth.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 6.17%.\n\nI think that makes sense because 6% compounded monthly is a bit less than 6.17% when compounded annually because the compounding happens more frequently, so the rate can be a bit higher to make up for the more frequent compounding.\n\nWait, no, actually, wait. More frequent compounding leads to a higher effective rate. So, if you have a higher compounding frequency, the effective rate is higher. So, 6% compounded monthly should have a higher effective rate than 6% compounded annually. So, 6.17% is higher than 6%, which is correct.\n\nBut wait, the question is, the bank account compounds monthly at 6%, and we need to find the equivalent annual rate. So, the effective annual rate is higher than 6%. So, 6.17% is the rate. So, 6.17% is the answer.\n\nWait, but let me verify with a different approach.\n\nSuppose I have $1000 in the account.\n\nAfter one year, with monthly compounding, the amount is:\n\n\\( A = 1000*(1 + 0.06/12)^{12} \\approx 1000*1.0616778 \\approx 1061.68 \\)\n\nSo, $1061.68 after one year.\n\nIf it were compounded annually at r%, then after one year, it would be:\n\n\\( A = 1000*(1 + r) \\)\n\nSetting them equal:\n\n\\( 1000*(1 + r) = 1000*1.0616778 \\)\n\nSo, \\( 1 + r = 1.0616778 \\), so \\( r = 0.0616778 \\), which is 6.16778%, so 6.17% when rounded to the nearest hundredth.\n\nTherefore, yes, 6.17% is correct.\n\nWait, but earlier, when I did the manual calculation, I thought it was 1.061855, but after doing it step by step, it was 1.0616778. So, perhaps my initial approximation was a bit off, but the accurate calculation is 1.0616778, which is 6.16778%.\n\nSo, 6.17% is the correct answer.\n\nTherefore, after this lengthy reasoning, I think the answer is 6.17%.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.17}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) that would give the same return as a 6% annual interest rate compounded monthly, we need to calculate the effective annual rate (EAR).\n\nThe formula for compound interest is:\n\\[ A = P \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n \\]\n\nFor a principal \\( P \\), the amount after one year with monthly compounding is:\n\\[ A_{\\text{monthly}} = P \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} \\]\n\nSimplifying the inside of the left side:\n\\[ 1 + \\frac{0.06}{12} = 1 + 0.005 = 1.005 \\]\n\nThus, the equation becomes:\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\nCalculating \\( (1.005)^{12} \\) step by step:\n\\[ 1.005^1 = 1.005 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^2 = 1.010025 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^3 \\approx 1.015075 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^4 \\approx 1.020150 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^5 \\approx 1.025252 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^6 \\approx 1.030377 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^7 \\approx 1.035546 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^8 \\approx 1.040812 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^9 \\approx 1.046041 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^{10} \\approx 1.051265 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^{11} \\approx 1.056644 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^{12} \\approx 1.0616778 \\]\n\nTherefore:\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} \\approx 1.0616778 \\]\n\nSo, the effective annual rate \\( r \\) is:\n\\[ r = 1.0616778 - 1 = 0.0616778 \\]\n\nConverting this to a percentage and rounding to the nearest hundredth:\n\\[ r \\approx 6.17\\% \\]\n\nThus, the equivalent annual interest rate is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6.17}\n\\]",
