      "full_cot": "I think I can do this using logarithms or a calculator, but since I'm just thinking, I'll try to remember that the formula for compound interest is unrelated to the effective annual rate. 6.17}\n\nWait, that was too quick. Let me actually compute \\( (1.005)^{12} \\) step by step. Let's recall that \\( (1 + \\frac{r}{n})^{n} \\) is the formula for the amount when compounded n times a year. So, for the effective annual rate, we have:\n\n\\[ (1 + \\frac{0.06}{12})^{12} \\]\n\nCompute \\( \\frac{0.06}{12} = 0.005 \\). So, it's \\( (1.005)^{12} \\). Let's compute this using the rule of exponentiation.\n\nAlternatively, I know that \\( \\ln(1.005) \\) is approximately 0.004975, so if I compute \\( e^{12 \\times 0.004975} \\), that's approximately \\( e^{0.0597} \\). Now, \\( e^{0.0597} \\) is approximately 1.0617.\n\nBut wait, let me verify that. Alternatively, I can compute \\( (1.005)^{12} \\) step by step:\n\nFirst, compute \\( 1.005^2 = 1.010025 \\).\n\nThen, \\( 1.010025^2 = 1.020150 \\).\n\nWait, hold on, that's squaring each time, but that's not the right approach for 12 multiplications. Alternatively, I can use the formula for compound interest step by step.\n\nAlternatively, I can use the formula \\( (1 + \\frac{r}{n})^{n} \\). So, for r = 0.06, n =12. So, compute 1.005^12.\n\nAlternatively, if I can recall that \\( \\ln(1.005) \\) is about 0.004975, so 12 times that is about 0.0597, and exponentiating that gives e^0.0597 ≈ 1.061678. So, approximately 1.061678.\n\nTherefore, 1 + r = 1.061678, so r ≈ 0.061678, which is 6.1678 percent. Rounded to the nearest hundredth, that's 6.17 percent.\n\nSo, r is approximately 6.17%.\n\nBut let me double-check to ensure I didn't make any mistakes.\n\nAlternatively, I can use the formula for effective annual rate:\n\n\\[ E = \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n - 1 \\]\n\nSo, plugging in the numbers:\n\nr = 0.06, n =12,\n\n\\[ E = (1 + 0.06/12)^{12} - 1 \\]\n\nWhich is the same as:\n\n\\[ E = (1.005)^{12} - 1 \\approx 1.061678 - 1 = 0.061678 \\]\n\nTherefore, E ≈ 6.1678%, which rounds to 6.17%.\n\nSo, yes, that seems correct. Therefore, the equivalent annual interest rate is approximately 6.17 percent.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.17}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) that would give the same return as a 6% annual interest rate compounded monthly, we need to calculate the effective annual rate (EAR). The compound interest formula is:\n\n\\[ A = P \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n \\]\n\nGiven:\n- The annual interest rate is 6%, so \\( r = 0.06 \\).\n- The interest is compounded monthly, so \\( n = 12 \\).\n\nWe need to find the effective annual rate \\( r \\) such that:\n\n\\[ \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\nFirst, compute the inside of the left side:\n\n\\[ 1 + \\frac{0.06}{12} = 1 + 0.005 = 1.005 \\]\n\nNext, compute \\( (1.005)^{12} \\):\n\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} \\approx 1.061678 \\]\n\nThus, the equation becomes:\n\n\\[ 1.061678 = 1 + r \\]\n\nSolving for \\( r \\):\n\n\\[ r \\approx 1.061678 - 1 = 0.061678 \\]\n\nConverting this to a percentage and rounding to the nearest hundredth:\n\n\\[ r \\approx 6.17\\% \\]\n\nTherefore, the equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.17}.",
