159275 ≈ 1.061677\n\nSo, \\( e^{0.05985} ≈ 1.061677 \\), which is very close to the step-by-step calculation. So, indeed, \\( (1.005)^{12} ≈ 1.061677 \\), so \\( r = 0.061677 \\), which is 6.1677%, or approximately 6.17%. So, my initial step-by-step calculation was perhaps off because of the limited number of terms I used each time. So, the accurate value is 6.1677%, which is approximately 6.17%.\n\nWait, but when I did the step-by-step multiplication, I only got up to 1.060525, which is a bit lower. Hmm, maybe I made an error in the step-by-step multiplication.\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nStarting with 1.005:\n\n1: 1.005\n\n2: 1.005 * 1.005 = 1.010025\n\n3: 1.010025 * 1.005 = 1.015075125\n\n4: 1.015075125 * 1.005 = 1.020150500\n\n5: 1.020150500 * 1.005 ≈ 1.025263005\n\n6: 1.025263005 * 1.005 ≈ 1.030520015\n\n7: 1.030520015 * 1.005 ≈ 1.035525015\n\n8: 1.035525015 * 1.005 ≈ 1.040525015\n\n9: 1.040525015 * 1.005 ≈ 1.045525015\n\n10: 1.045525015 * 1.005 ≈ 1.050525015\n\n11: 1.050525015 * 1.005 ≈ 1.055525015\n\n12: 1.055525015 * 1.005 ≈ 1.060525015\n\nSo, after 12 multiplications, it's approximately 1.060525. But according to the exponentiation method, it's about 1.061677. There is a discrepancy here. So, why is that?\n\nWait, perhaps in my step-by-step multiplication, I didn't carry all the decimal places, leading to a lower result. Because each time I approximate, the errors can accumulate. So, the exact value is approximately 1.061677, but my manual multiplication after 12 steps is giving me 1.060525. So, which one is more accurate?\n\nWait, let's use a calculator method for computing 1.005^12.\n\nLet me compute 1.005^2: 1.005*1.005 = 1.010025\n\n1.005^4: (1.010025)^2. Let's compute that: 1.010025*1.010025.\n\nMultiply 1.010025 by itself:\n\n1 * 1 = 1\n\n1 * 0.010025 = 0.010025\n\n0.010025 * 1 = 0.010025\n\n0.010025 * 0.010025 = approx 0.0001005\n\nAdding them up:\n\n1 + 0.010025 + 0.010025 + 0.0001005 = 1 + 0.02005 + 0.0001005 ≈ 1.0201505\n\nSo, 1.005^4 ≈ 1.0201505\n\nNo