      "full_cot": "So, \\( e^{0.597} \\) is approximately equal to... } Wait, hold on, I need to compute \\( (1.005)^{12} \\) properly. Maybe I should do it step by step. Let me recall that \\( 1.005^12 \\) can be calculated by multiplying 1.005 twelve times. But that would take too long. Alternatively, I can use the formula for compound interest's effective annual rate: \\[ (1 + \\frac{r}{n})^{n} - 1 = r_{\\text{effective}} \\] Wait, but that's not exactly. Let me correct that. The formula is: \\[ (1 + \\frac{r}{n})^{n} = e^{r} \\] when n approaches infinity. But for finite n, it's an approximation. Hmm, maybe another way. Alternatively, I can use the natural logarithm and exponential to calculate it.\n\nLet me try this: \\[ \\ln(1.005^{12}) = 12 \\times \\ln(1.005) \\] So, ln(1.005) is approximately 0.004979. Multiply that by 12: 0.004979 * 12 = 0.059748. Then, exponentiate that: e^{0.059748} ≈ 1.06167. So, \\( 1.005^{12} ≈ 1.06167 \\). Therefore, the effective annual rate is approximately 6.167%. So, rounding to the nearest hundredth, that would be 6.17%. Therefore, the value of r is approximately 6.17 percent.\n\nWait, let me confirm this another way. I remember that when compounding more frequently, the effective rate increases. So, 6% compounded monthly should have a slightly higher effective rate than 6% compounded annually, which makes sense because 6.17% is higher than 6%. So, that seems consistent.\n\nAlternatively, if I use a calculator for better precision, \\( (1.005)^{12} \\) is approximately 1.061678. So, subtracting 1 gives 0.061678, which is 6.1678%, which rounds to 6.17%. So, yes, that seems correct.\n\nSo, I think that the equivalent annual interest rate is approximately 6.17%. Therefore, r is 6.17, so the answer is 6.17, and in the box, it should be \\boxed{6.17}\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.17}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) percent that would give the same return as a 6 percent annual interest rate compounded monthly, we use the concept of effective annual rate (EAR).\n\nThe formula for compound interest is:\n\\[ A = P \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n \\]\n\nFor monthly compounding, the interest rate is 6% (or 0.06) compounded monthly, which means \\( n = 12 \\). The formula for the amount after one year with monthly compounding is:\n\\[ A_{\\text{monthly}} = P \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} \\]\n\nFor annual compounding, the interest rate is \\( r \\) percent, so the formula for the amount after one year is:\n\\[ A_{\\text{annual}} = P (1 + r) \\]\n\nSince both amounts are equal after one year, we set them equal:\n\\[ P \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = P (1 + r) \\]\n\nThe principal \\( P \\) cancels out, leaving:\n\\[ \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\nCalculating the inside of the left side:\n\\[ 1 + \\frac{0.06}{12} = 1 + 0.005 = 1.005 \\]\n\nSo, the equation becomes:\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\nUsing logarithms to approximate \\( (1.005)^{12} \\):\n\\[ \\ln(1.005^{12}) = 12 \\times \\ln(1.005) \\]\n\\[ \\ln(1.005) \\approx 0.004979 \\]\n\\[ 12 \\times 0.004979 = 0.059748 \\]\n\\[ e^{0.059748} \\approx 1.06167 \\]\n\nThus:\n\\[ 1.06167 = 1 + r \\]\n\\[ r \\approx 0.06167 \\]\n\nConverting this to a percentage and rounding to the nearest hundredth:\n\\[ r \\approx 6.17\\% \\]\n\nTherefore, the equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.17}.",
