erefore, 1.0610, subtract 1, gives 0.0610, so 6.10%.\n\nAlternatively, if I use more accurate methods, perhaps a calculator would give a slightly different result, but given that I'm only a human and can't compute infinitely, 6.10% is precise to the nearest hundredth.\n\nTherefore, the equivalent annual interest rate is approximately 6.10%.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.10}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) that would give the same return as a bank account with an annual interest rate of 6 percent compounded monthly, we need to calculate the effective annual rate (EAR).\n\nThe formula for the effective annual rate is:\n\\[ \\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + \\frac{r_{\\text{nominal}}}{n}\\right)^n - 1 \\]\nwhere \\( r_{\\text{nominal}} \\) is the nominal annual interest rate and \\( n \\) is the number of compounding periods per year.\n\nGiven:\n- \\( r_{\\text{nominal}} = 6\\% = 0.06 \\)\n- \\( n = 12 \\) (since it compounds monthly)\n\nSubstituting the values into the formula:\n\\[ \\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} - 1 \\]\n\\[ \\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + 0.005\\right)^{12} - 1 \\]\n\\[ \\text{EAR} = (1.005)^{12} - 1 \\]\n\nCalculating \\( (1.005)^{12} \\):\nUsing step-by-step multiplication or logarithms, we find:\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} \\approx 1.061008 \\]\n\nThus,\n\\[ \\text{EAR} = 1.061008 - 1 = 0.061008 \\]\n\nConverting this to a percentage:\n\\[ \\text{EAR} \\approx 6.10\\% \\]\n\nTherefore, the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{6.10} \\]",
