      "full_cot": "1.005^3 = 1.010025 * 1.005 ≈ 1.016075125 }\n\nWait, let me do it more accurately. Let me just compute \\( (1.005)^{12} \\). Maybe I can calculate it step by step:\n\n1.005^1 = 1.005\n\n1.005^2 = 1.005 * 1.005 = 1.010025\n\n1.005^3 = 1.010025 * 1.005 ≈ 1.015075125\n\n1.005^4 ≈ 1.015075125 * 1.005 ≈ 1.020150781\n\n1.005^5 ≈ 1.020150781 * 1.005 ≈ 1.025322195\n\n1.005^6 ≈ 1.025322195 * 1.005 ≈ 1.030376367\n\n1.005^7 ≈ 1.030376367 * 1.005 ≈ 1.035461294\n\n1.005^8 ≈ 1.035461294 * 1.005 ≈ 1.040604998\n\n1.005^9 ≈ 1.040604998 * 1.005 ≈ 1.045762049\n\n1.005^10 ≈ 1.045762049 * 1.005 ≈ 1.051010075\n\n1.005^11 ≈ 1.051010075 * 1.005 ≈ 1.056177084\n\n1.005^12 ≈ 1.056177084 * 1.005 ≈ 1.061678175\n\nSo, \\( (1.005)^{12} \\approx 1.061678175 \\)\n\nTherefore, \\( 1 + r = 1.061678175 \\)\n\nSo, subtracting 1: \\( r = 0.061678175 \\)\n\nTo convert that to a percentage, multiply by 100: 6.1678175%\n\nRounding to the nearest hundredth: 6.17%\n\nSo, the equivalent annual interest rate is approximately 6.17%.\n\nWait, let me verify if I did all the multiplications correctly. Sometimes when I compute step by step, I might make a mistake.\n\nAlternatively, I can use the formula for EAR:\n\nEAR = (1 + (nominal rate / number of periods))^(number of periods) - 1\n\nWhich is exactly what I did. So, 1 + 0.06/12 = 1.005, raised to 12, minus 1 gives the EAR.\n\nSo, 1.005^12 is about 1.061678, so EAR is 6.1678%, which is 6.17% when rounded to the nearest hundredth.\n\nI think that's correct. So, the answer is 6.17.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.17}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) percent for Dr. Fu Manchu's bank account that compounds monthly at an annual interest rate of 6 percent, we need to calculate the effective annual rate (EAR).\n\nThe compound interest formula for one year is:\n\\[ A = P \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n \\]\n\nFor monthly compounding:\n\\[ A_{\\text{monthly}} = P \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} \\]\n\nFor annual compounding:\n\\[ A_{\\text{annual}} = P (1 + r) \\]\n\nSince both amounts are equal after one year:\n\\[ P \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = P (1 + r) \\]\n\nCanceling \\( P \\) from both sides:\n\\[ \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\nCalculating the inside of the left side:\n\\[ 1 + \\frac{0.06}{12} = 1 + 0.005 = 1.005 \\]\n\nSo, the equation becomes:\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\nCalculating \\( (1.005)^{12} \\):\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} \\approx 1.061678175 \\]\n\nThus:\n\\[ 1 + r = 1.061678175 \\]\n\\[ r = 0.061678175 \\]\n\nConverting to a percentage and rounding to the nearest hundredth:\n\\[ r \\approx 6.17\\% \\]\n\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.17}.",
