 the formula for continuous compounding, but in this case, it's compounded monthly, so not continuous. But I think my approach is correct.\n\nWait, just to make sure, let me use the formula for effective annual rate, which is indeed:\n\n\\[ (1 + \\frac{r}{n})^n - 1 \\]\n\nWhich is exactly what I computed. So, yes, 6.066% rounds to 6.07%.\n\nAlternatively, I can use logarithms. Let me try that approach to verify.\n\nTake the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n\\[ \\ln\\left( (1.005)^{12} \\right) = \\ln(1 + r) \\]\n\nSo,\n\n\\[ 12 \\times \\ln(1.005) = \\ln(1 + r) \\]\n\nCompute \\( \\ln(1.005) \\). I remember that \\( \\ln(1 + x) ≈ x - x^2/2 + x^3/3 - x^4/4 + ... \\) for small x. Since 0.005 is small, let's use this approximation.\n\nSo,\n\n\\( \\ln(1.005) ≈ 0.005 - (0.005)^2 / 2 + (0.005)^3 / 3 - (0.005)^4 / 4 + ... \\)\n\nCompute up to the x^3 term for better approximation.\n\nFirst term: 0.005\n\nSecond term: - (0.000025)/2 = -0.0000125\n\nThird term: + (0.000000125)/3 ≈ +0.000000041666...\n\nFourth term: - (0.00000000015625)/4 ≈ -0.0000000000390625\n\nSo, adding these up:\n\n0.005 - 0.0000125 + 0.000000041666 - 0.0000000000390625 ≈ 0.004987541625\n\nSo, \\( \\ln(1.005) ≈ 0.0049875 \\)\n\nTherefore, 12 * 0.0049875 ≈ 0.05985\n\nSo, \\( \\ln(1 + r) ≈ 0.05985 \\)\n\nTherefore, \\( 1 + r = e^{0.05985} \\)\n\nCompute \\( e^{0.05985} \\). I know that \\( e^{0.06} ≈ 1.06183654 \\). Since 0.05985 is very close to 0.06, let's compute it as:\n\nLet me write 0.05985 = 0.06 - 0.00015\n\nSo, \\( e^{0.05985} = e^{0.06 - 0.00015} = e^{0.06} \\times e^{-0.00015} \\)\n\nWe know that \\( e^{-0.00015} ≈ 1 - 0.00015 + (0.00015)^2 / 2 - ... ≈ 0.99985 \\)\n\nTherefore, \\( e^{0.05985} ≈ 1.06183654 \\times 0.99985 ≈ 1.06183654 - (1.06183654 \\times 0.00015) \\)\n\nCompute 1.06183654 * 0.00015:\n\n≈ 0.00015927546\n\nSo, subtracting:\n\n1.06183654 - 0.00015927546 ≈ 1.061677264\n\nTherefore, \\( 1 + r ≈ 1.061677264 \\), so \\( r ≈ 0.061677264 \\), which is 6.1677264%, which rounds to 6.1