      "full_cot": "1.005^10 ≈ 1.045461025 * 1.005 ≈ 1.050529126 6.17\\%}\n\nWait, but in my step-by-step calculation, 1.005^12 is approximately 1.061678. So, 1 + r = 1.061678, so r is approximately 0.061678, which is 6.1678%. So, rounded to the nearest hundredth is 6.17%. So, the equivalent annual rate is approximately 6.17%. Therefore, the answer is 6.17.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.17}.\n\\\n\nWait, actually, let me double-check my calculation because I initially thought 1.005^12 is around 1.061678, which would make r = 0.061678 or 6.1678%, so 6.17% when rounded to the nearest hundredth. However, sometimes when dealing with financial rates, people use more precise calculations. Let me verify using a more accurate method. I can use the formula for effective annual rate directly.\n\nThe effective annual rate (EAR) can be calculated using the formula:\n\n\\[ \\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n - 1 \\]\n\nWhere r is the annual nominal interest rate, and n is the number of compounding periods per year. In this case, r = 6% or 0.06, and n = 12 because it's compounded monthly.\n\nSo, plugging in the numbers:\n\n\\[ \\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} - 1 \\]\n\\[ \\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + 0.005\\right)^{12} - 1 \\]\n\\[ \\text{EAR} = (1.005)^{12} - 1 \\]\n\nNow, calculating (1.005)^12 precisely. I can use logarithms or perhaps a better approximation. Alternatively, I can use the formula for compound interest again step by step.\n\nAlternatively, using the rule of 72 or something, but perhaps better to use a calculator approach.\n\nBut since I don't have a calculator, let me try to compute it more accurately. Let me use natural logarithms and exponentials.\n\nWe have:\n\n\\( \\ln(1.005) \\approx 0.004979 \\)\n\nSo, \\( \\ln((1.005)^{12}) = 12 \\times 0.004979 = 0.059738 \\)\n\nTherefore, \\( (1.005)^{12} = e^{0.059738} \\)\n\nNow, e^0.059738 can be approximated.\n\nWe know that:\n\ne^0.06 = 1.06183654