36 * 1 = 1.040707036\n\n1.040707036 * 0.005 = 0.00520353518\n\nAdd: 1.040707036 + 0.00520353518 ≈ 1.045910571\n\n1.005^10 = 1.045910571 * 1.005\n\n1.045910571 * 1 = 1.045910571\n\n1.045910571 * 0.005 = 0.005229552855\n\nAdd: 1.045910571 + 0.005229552855 ≈ 1.051140123\n\n1.005^11 = 1.051140123 * 1.005\n\n1.051140123 * 1 = 1.051140123\n\n1.051140123 * 0.005 = 0.005255700615\n\nAdd: 1.051140123 + 0.005255700615 ≈ 1.056395823\n\n1.005^12 = 1.056395823 * 1.005\n\n1.056395823 * 1 = 1.056395823\n\n1.056395823 * 0.005 = 0.005281979115\n\nAdd: 1.056395823 + 0.005281979115 ≈ 1.061677802\n\nSo, after computing step by step, 1.005^12 is approximately 1.061677802.\n\nTherefore, \\( r = 1.061677802 - 1 = 0.061677802 \\), which is approximately 0.06168 or 6.168%. Rounding to the nearest hundredth, that's 6.17%. So, I think that's correct. So, the equivalent annual rate is 6.17%.\n\nAlternatively, if I use the formula for the effective annual rate, which is \\( (1 + \\frac{r}{n})^n - 1 \\), plugging in r = 0.06 and n = 12, we get the same result.\n\nI was a bit confused at first when I saw the problem, but breaking it down step by step helped. I had to remember how compound interest works and how the effective annual rate is calculated. It's important to note that when compounding more frequently, the interest earned in the compounding periods can start earning interest itself, leading to a higher overall amount than simple interest or less frequent compounding.\n\nI think I might have made a mistake if I had just directly subtracted the two rates, like 6% minus something, but that's not the case. Instead, it's about the growth factor over the year, which depends on the number of compounding periods.\n\nSo, in summary, by using the compound interest formula and setting the amounts equal for both compounding methods over the same period, I was able to solve for the equivalent annual interest rate. It's about 6.17%, which is slightly higher than the nominal 6% due to the compounding effect.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.17}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) that would give the same return if it were compounded annually instead of monthly, we need to calculate the effective annual rate (EAR). The nominal annual interest rate is 6%, compounded monthly.\n\nThe formula for compound interest is:\n\\[ A = P \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n \\]\n\nFor monthly compounding, the formula becomes:\n\\[ A_{\\text{monthly}} = P \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} \\]\n\nFor annual compounding, the formula is:\n\\[ A_{\\text{annual}} = P (1 + r) \\]\n\nSince both amounts after one year should be equal:\n\\[ P \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = P (1 + r) \\]\n\nThe principal \\( P \\) cancels out, so we have:\n\\[ \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\nCalculating the inside of the left side:\n\\[ 1 + \\frac{0.06}{12} = 1 + 0.005 = 1.005 \\]\n\nThus, the equation becomes:\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\nCalculating \\( (1.005)^{12} \\) step by step:\n\\[ 1.005^{12} \\approx 1.061677802 \\]\n\nTherefore:\n\\[ r = 1.061677802 - 1 = 0.061677802 \\]\n\nRounding to the nearest hundredth, we get:\n\\[ r \\approx 0.0617 \\]\n\nConverting this to a percentage:\n\\[ r \\approx 6.17\\% \\]\n\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.17}.",
