made a mistake in the initial step-by-step. Wait, 1.005^12 is definitely approximately 1.06168, so 6.168%, so 6.17%. So, the correct value is approximately 6.17%.\n\nBut to be thorough, let me check using logarithms.\n\nWe can use the formula \\( \\ln(1 + \\frac{r}{n})^{n} = n \\ln(1 + \\frac{r}{n}) \\).\n\nSo, \\( \\ln(1.005) \\approx 0.004975 \\).\n\nTherefore, \\( 12 \\times 0.004975 = 0.0597 \\).\n\nSo, \\( e^{0.0597} \\approx 1.0617 \\).\n\nTherefore, \\( r = 1.0617 - 1 = 0.0617 \\), which is 6.17%.\n\nSo, 6.17% is the correct value, so rounding to the nearest hundredth, it's 6.17%.\n\nWait, but when I did the step-by-step multiplication, I got 1.06168, which is 6.168%, so 6.17% when rounded to the nearest hundredth.\n\nWait, so my initial calculation was wrong because I stopped at 1.060660127, but that was due to miscalculating the step-by-step, because when I did it more precisely, I got 1.06168.\n\nTherefore, the correct effective annual rate is approximately 6.17%.\n\nSo, the answer is 6.17%, which is 6.17.\n\nBut wait, let me confirm once more using another method.\n\nAlternatively, I can use the formula for compound interest:\n\n\\( A = P(1 + \\frac{r}{n})^{nt} \\)\n\nBut here, since we're comparing after one year, t=1, so:\n\n\\( A_{monthly} = P(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12})^{12} \\)\n\n\\( A_{annual} = P(1 + r) \\)\n\nSetting them equal:\n\n\\( (1 + 0.005)^{12} = 1 + r \\)\n\nSo, calculate \\( (1.005)^{12} \\).\n\nI think another way to compute this is using the formula for compound interest and the rule of 72, but that might not be precise.\n\nAlternatively, use the Taylor series expansion for \\( (1 + x)^n \\) where x is small.\n\nSo, \\( (1 + x)^n ≈ 1 + nx + \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}x^2 + \\cdots \\)\n\nHere, x = 0.005, n = 12.\n\nSo, \\( (1.005)^{12} ≈ 1 + 12*0.005 + (12*11)/2*(0.005)^2 \\)\n\nCompute each term:\n\nFirst term: 1\n\nSecond term: 12 * 0.005 = 0.06\n\nThird term: (132)/2 * 0.000025 = 66 * 0.000025 = 0.00165\n\nSo, adding these up: 1 + 0.06 + 0.00165 = 1.06165\n\nTherefore, \\( (1.005)^{12} ≈ 1.06165 \\), so \\( r ≈ 0.06165 \\), which is 6.165%, which is approximately 6.17% when rounded to the nearest hundredth.\n\nSo, that's consistent with the earlier result.\n\nTherefore, the effective annual rate is approximately 6.17%.\n\nSo, the answer is 6.17.\n\nBut wait, the problem says to give the answer to the nearest hundredth. 6.17 is already to the nearest hundredth. So, 6.17%.\n\nBut in the initial calculation, when I did step-by-step without enough precision, I thought it was 6.06%, but that was incorrect. After more precise calculation, it's 6.17%.\n\nTherefore, the correct value is 6.17%.\n\nSo, I think I confused myself initially, but now I'm confident that the correct answer is 6.17%.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.17}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) that would give the same return if compounded annually instead of monthly, we need to calculate the effective annual rate (EAR) for the given nominal rate of 6% compounded monthly.\n\nThe compound interest formula is:\n\\[ A = P \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n \\]\n\nGiven:\n- The nominal rate \\( r = 6\\% = 0.06 \\)\n- The number of compounding periods per year \\( n = 12 \\)\n\nWe set the amount after one year with monthly compounding equal to the amount after one year with annual compounding:\n\\[ P \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = P (1 + r) \\]\n\nThe principal \\( P \\) cancels out, leaving:\n\\[ \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\nSimplifying inside the parentheses:\n\\[ 1 + \\frac{0.06}{12} = 1 + 0.005 = 1.005 \\]\n\nThus, we need to compute:\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} \\]\n\nUsing step-by-step calculation or logarithms, we find:\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} \\approx 1.0617 \\]\n\nSubtracting 1 from both sides gives the effective annual rate:\n\\[ r = 1.0617 - 1 = 0.0617 \\]\n\nConverting this to a percentage and rounding to the nearest hundredth:\n\\[ r \\approx 6.17\\% \\]\n\nTherefore, the equivalent annual interest rate is:\n\\[ \\boxed{6.17} \\]",
