    {
      "full_cot": "So, 1.06066 is approximately 1.0607 when rounded to four decimal places, but since we need to find r to the nearest hundredth, we can take 1.06066 as approximately 1.0607, so r is 0.607, which is 6.07%. 6.07\\%}\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.07\\%}.\n\\\n\nTo find the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) for a bank account that compounds monthly at an annual interest rate of 6 percent, we need to calculate the effective annual rate (EAR). The formula for compound interest is:\n\n\\[ A = P \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n \\]\n\nFor one year with monthly compounding, we have:\n\n\\[ A_{\\text{monthly}} = P \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} \\]\n\nFor annual compounding, the amount after one year is:\n\n\\[ A_{\\text{annual}} = P (1 + r) \\]\n\nSince both amounts are equal for the same principal \\( P \\), we set them equal:\n\n\\[ \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\nCalculating the inside of the left side:\n\n\\[ 1 + \\frac{0.06}{12} = 1 + 0.005 = 1.005 \\]\n\nSo, the equation becomes:\n\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\nCalculating \\( (1.005)^{12} \\):\n\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} \\approx 1.06066 \\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[ 1 + r \\approx 1.06066 \\]\n\\[ r \\approx 0.06066 \\]\n\\[ r \\approx 6.07\\% \\]\n\nTherefore, the equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.07\\%}.",
