imately 1.061964. Which is correct? Hmm, maybe I made a mistake in my earlier step-by-step.\n\nWait, let me recount:\n\n1.005^1 = 1.005\n\n1.005^2 = 1.010025\n\n1.005^3 = 1.015075\n\n1.005^4 ≈ 1.020150\n\n1.005^5 ≈ 1.025251\n\n1.005^6 ≈ 1.030301\n\n1.005^7 ≈ 1.035355\n\n1.005^8 ≈ 1.040408\n\n1.005^9 ≈ 1.045461\n\n1.005^10 ≈ 1.050525\n\n1.005^11 ≈ 1.055600\n\n1.005^12 ≈ 1.060660\n\nWait, but when I did the step-by-step calculation, I ended up with 1.061964. Hmm, so which is correct? I must have made a mistake somewhere. Maybe I miscalculated in the second method.\n\nWait, in the second step-by-step, I think I added 0.056728 * 1.005 as 0.056964, but let's compute that accurately:\n\n0.056728 * 1.005:\n\n0.05 * 1.005 = 0.05025\n\n0.006728 * 1.005 ≈ 0.006772\n\nAdding them: 0.05025 + 0.006772 ≈ 0.057022\n\nSo, 1.005 + 0.057022 ≈ 1.062022, not 1.061964.\n\nWait, so 1.005^12 ≈ 1.062022\n\nWait, but hold on, let's verify that:\n\n1.005^12 = (1.005)^12\n\nLet me use logarithms for a more accurate calculation.\n\nCompute ln(1.005):\n\nln(1.005) ≈ 0.004979\n\nMultiply by 12: 0.004979 * 12 ≈ 0.059748\n\nSo, e^0.059748 ≈ ?\n\nCompute e^0.059748.\n\nWe know that e^0.06 ≈ 1.06183654.\n\nSince 0.059748 is slightly less than 0.06, so e^0.059748 ≈ 1.06183654 - (0.06 - 0.059748)* derivative at x=0.06.\n\nThe derivative of e^x is e^x, so at x=0.06, the derivative is 1.06183654.\n\nSo, approximate e^0.059748 ≈ e^0.06 - (0.000252)*(1.06183654) ≈ 1.06183654 - 0.000267 ≈ 1.06157.\n\nAlternatively, maybe it's better to use a calculator, but since I don't have one, perhaps accept that 1.005^12 is approximately 1.06157 to 1.0616.\n\nWait, but my step-by-step gave me 1.062022, and the logarithm method gave me approximately 1.06157.\n\nHmm, this discrepancy is making me unsure. Maybe I need to accept that my step-by-step calculation is slightly off because of rounding errors. So, 1.005^12 is approximately 1.06157, which would make r ≈ 6.157%. But that contradicts the earlier step-by-step.\n\nWait, maybe I need to use a more accurate method. Let me recall that (1 + r/n)^n can be expanded using the binomial theorem or approximated. Alternatively, I can use the formula for the effective annual rate:\n\nEAR = (1 + r/n)^n - 1\n\nSo, with r = 0.06, n = 12,\n\nEAR = (1 + 0.06/12)^12 - 1 ≈ (1.005)^12 - 1 ≈ 1.06157 - 1 ≈ 0.06157, so 6.157%.\n\nBut when I computed step-by-step, I had 1.062022, which would be 6.2022%. So, which is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the step-by-step.\n\nWait, let me compute 1.005^12 again step by step with more precise calculations.\n\nStarting with 1.005:\n\n1.005^1 = 1.005\n\n1.005^2 = 1.005 * 1.005 = 1.010025\n\n1.005^3 = 1.010025 * 1.005\n\nLet me compute 1.010025 * 1.005:\n\nMultiply 1.010025 * 1 = 1.010025\n\nMultiply 1.010025 * 0.005 = 0.005050125\n\nAdd them: 1.010025 + 0.005050125 = 1.015075125\n\n1.005^3 = 1.015075125\n\n1.005^4 = 1.015075125 * 1.005\n\nCompute 1.015075125 * 1.005:\n\n1.015075125 * 1 = 1.015075125\n\n1.015075125 * 0.005 = 0.005075375625\n\nAdd them: 1.015075125 + 0.005075375625 ≈ 1.020150500625\n\n1.005^4 ≈ 1.020150500625\n\n1.005^5 = 1.020150500625 * 1.005\n\nCompute:\n\n1.020150500625 * 1 = 1.020150500625\n\n1.020150500625 * 0.005 = 0.0051007528125\n\nAdd them: 1.020150500625 + 0.0051007528125 ≈ 1.0252512534375\n\n1.005^5 ≈ 1.0252512534375\n\n1.005^6 = 1.0252512534375 * 1.005\n\nCompute:\n\n1.0252512534375 * 1 = 1.0252512534375\n\n1.0252512534375 * 0.005 = 0.00512625671875\n\nAdd them: 1.0252512534375 + 0.00512625671875 ≈ 1.03037751015625\n\n1.005^6 ≈ 1.03037751015625\n\n1.005^7 = 1.03037751015625 * 1.005\n\nCompute:\n\n1.03037751015625 * 1 = 1.03037751015625\n\n1.03037751015625 * 0.005 = 0.00515188755078125\n\nAdd them: 1.03037751015625 + 0.00515188755078125 ≈ 1.0355293977066406\n\n1.005^7 ≈ 1.0355293977066406\n\n1.005^8 = 1.0355293977066406 * 1.005\n\nCompute:\n\n1.0355293977066406 * 1 = 1.0355293977066406\n\n1.0355293977066406 * 0.005 = 0.005177646985320303\n\nAdd them: 1.0355293977066406 + 0.005177646985320303 ≈ 1.0407070446919609\n\n1.005^8 ≈ 1.0407070446919609\n\n1.005^9 = 1.0407070446919609 * 1.005\n\nCompute:\n\n1.0407070446919609 * 1 = 1.0407070446919609\n\n1.0407070446919609 * 0.005 = 0.005203535224\n\nAdd them: 1.0407070446919609 + 0.005203535224 ≈ 1.0459105799159609\n\n1.005^9 ≈ 1.0459105799159609\n\n1.005^10 = 1.0459105799159609 * 1.005\n\nCompute:\n\n1.0459105799159609 * 1 = 1.0459105799159609\n\n1.0459105799159609 * 0.005 = 0.005229552899\n\nAdd them: 1.0459105799159609 + 0.005229552899 ≈ 1.0511401328149609\n\n1.005^10 ≈ 1.0511401328149609\n\n1.005^11 = 1.0511401328149609 * 1.005\n\nCompute:\n\n1.0511401328149609 * 1 = 1.0511401328149609\n\n1.0511401328149609 * 0.005 = 0.005255700664\n\nAdd them: 1.0511401328149609 + 0.005255700664 ≈ 1.0563958334799609\n\n1.005^11 ≈ 1.0563958334799609\n\n1.005^12 = 1.0563958334799609 * 1.005\n\nCompute:\n\n1.0563958334799609 * 1 = 1.0563958334799609\n\n1.0563958334799609 * 0.005 = 0.0052819791669945\n\nAdd them: 1.0563958334799609 + 0.0052819791669945 ≈ 1.0616778126469554\n\nSo, 1.005^12 ≈ 1.0616778126469554, which is approximately 1.06168.\n\nTherefore, \\( 1.06168 = 1 + r \\), so \\( r = 0.06168 \\) or 6.168%.\n\nRounded to the nearest hundredth, that's 6.17%.\n\nWait, but earlier, when I used logarithms, I had 0.059748, and e^0.059748 ≈ 1.06157, so r ≈ 0.06157, which is 6.157%. So, now I have two different results: 6.17% and 6.16%. Which is more accurate?\n\nWait, actually, perhaps the logarithm method is more precise because it's less prone to compounding errors in step-by-step multiplication. So, let me compute it more accurately.\n\nCompute ln(1.005):\n\nln(1.005) = ?\n\nUsing Taylor series expansion for ln(1+x) around x=0: ln(1+x) = x - x^2/2 + x^3/3 - x^4/4 + ...\n\nSo, ln(1.005) = 0.005 - (0.005)^2 / 2 + (0.005)^3 / 3 - (0.005)^4 / 4 + ...\n\nCompute up to the fourth term:\n\n0.005 = 0.005\n\n(0.005)^2 = 0.000025, so -0.000025 / 2 = -0.0000125\n\n(0.005)^3 = 0.000000125, so +0.000000125 / 3 ≈ +0.0000000416667\n\n(0.005)^4 = 0.000000000625, so -0.000000000625 / 4 ≈ -0.00000000015625\n\nAdding these:\n\n0.005 - 0.0000125 = 0.0049875\n\n0.0049875 + 0.0000000416667 ≈ 0.0049875416667\n\n0.0049875416667 - 0.00000000015625 ≈ 0.0049875415104\n\nSo, ln(1.005) ≈ 0.0049875415104\n\nMultiply by 12:\n\n0.0049875415104 * 12 ≈ 0.0598491366148\n\nSo, ln(A) = 0.0598491366148\n\nTherefore, A = e^{0.0598491366148}\n\nCompute e^{0.0598491366148}:\n\nWe know that e^{0.06} ≈ 1.061836545\n\nSo, let me compute e^{0.0598491366148} using the Taylor series around x=0.06.\n\nLet me set x = 0.0598491366148\n\nLet me write x = 0.06 - 0.0001508633852\n\nSo, e^{0.06 - 0.0001508633852} = e^{0.06} * e^{-0.0001508633852}\n\nCompute e^{-0.0001508633852}:\n\nAgain, using Taylor series: e^{-y} ≈ 1 - y + y^2/2 - y^3/6 + ...\n\nSo, y = 0.0001508633852\n\nCompute:\n\n1 - 0.0001508633852 = 0.9998491366148\n\n(0.0001508633852)^2 / 2 ≈ 0.0000001136 / 2 ≈ 0.0000000568\n\n(0.0001508633852)^3 / 6 ≈ 0.0000000172 / 6 ≈ 0.00000000287\n\nSo, e^{-y} ≈ 0.9998491366148 - 0.0000000568 + 0.00000000287 ≈ 0.99984908277\n\nTherefore, e^{0.0598491366148} ≈ e^{0.06} * e^{-y} ≈ 1.061836545 * 0.99984908277 ≈\n\nCompute 1.061836545 * 0.99984908277:\n\n1.061836545 * 0.99984908277 ≈ 1.061836545 - (1.061836545 * 0.0001508633852)\n\nCompute 1.061836545 * 0.0001508633852 ≈ 0.00016045\n\nTherefore, 1.061836545 - 0.00016045 ≈ 1.0616761\n\nSo, A ≈ 1.0616761, so 1.0616761 = 1 + r, so r ≈ 0.0616761, which is approximately 6.1676%, which is 6.17% when rounded to the nearest hundredth.\n\nWait, so that aligns with the step-by-step computation earlier, giving 1.0616778, so 6.17%.\n\nSo, that must be the correct value.\n\nBut in the logarithm method, I first thought it was 6.157%, but that was due to an incorrect approximation. The more accurate computation gave 6.17%.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps use the formula:\n\nEAR = (1 + (r/n))^n - 1\n\nSo, plugging in r = 6% and n =12,\n\nEAR = (1 + 0.06/12)^12 -1 = (1.005)^12 -1 ≈ 1.0616778 -1 ≈ 0.0616778, so 6.16778%, which is 6.17% when rounded.\n\nSo, the answer is 6.17%.\n\nWait, so earlier I was confused because of a miscalculation in step-by-step, but upon more precise computation, both methods agree on 6.17%.\n\nTherefore, the equivalent annual rate r is 6.17%.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.17}.\n\\boxed{6.17}\n</think>\n\nTo find the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) that would give the same return as a 6% annual interest rate compounded monthly, we need to calculate the effective annual rate (EAR).\n\nThe formula for compound interest is:\n\\[ A = P \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n \\]\n\nFor the monthly compounding case:\n\\[ A_{\\text{monthly}} = P \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} \\]\n\nFor the annual compounding case:\n\\[ A_{\\text{annual}} = P \\left(1 + r \\right) \\]\n\nSince both amounts are equal after one year, we set them equal:\n\\[ P \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = P \\left(1 + r \\right) \\]\n\nThe principal \\( P \\) cancels out:\n\\[ \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\nFirst, compute the inside of the left side:\n\\[ 1 + \\frac{0.06}{12} = 1 + 0.005 = 1.005 \\]\n\nNow, calculate \\( (1.005)^{12} \\):\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} \\approx 1.0616778 \\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\\[ 1.0616778 = 1 + r \\]\n\\[ r = 1.0616778 - 1 \\]\n\\[ r \\approx 0.0616778 \\]\n\nConverting this to a percentage and rounding to the nearest hundredth:\n\\[ r \\approx 6.17\\% \\]\n\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\(\\boxed{6.17}\\).",
