1.005\n\nCompute:\n\n1 * 1.0563958327011 = 1.0563958327011\n\n0.005 * 1.0563958327011 = 0.0052819791635055\n\nAdding together: 1.0563958327011 + 0.0052819791635055 ≈ 1.0616778118646\n\nSo, 1.005^12 ≈ 1.0616778118646\n\nThus, r = 1.0616778118646 - 1 = 0.0616778118646, which is approximately 6.16778118646%. So, rounding to the nearest hundredth, that is 6.17%.\n\nBut earlier, when I used the formula with logarithms, I got 6.166%, which rounds to 6.17%. So, both methods give me approximately 6.17%.\n\nWait, but earlier in my step-by-step, I thought 1.005^12 was 1.06066, but now I see that actually, 1.005^12 is approximately 1.0616778, which is around 1.06168. So, I think my initial step-by-step was wrong because I must have miscalculated 1.005^12 as 1.06066, but in reality, it's about 1.06168. So, this is where I might have messed up earlier.\n\nTherefore, the correct effective annual rate is approximately 6.17%.\n\nWait, so the answer should be 6.17%, which is approximately 6.17 percent when rounded to the nearest hundredth. Therefore, the equivalent annual rate r is 6.17%.\n\nBut let me confirm this with the formula for effective annual rate. The formula is:\n\n\\[ \\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n - 1 \\]\n\nWhere:\n- r is the nominal annual interest rate (6% or 0.06)\n- n is the number of compounding periods per year (12 for monthly)\n\nSo, substituting:\n\n\\[ \\text{EAR} = (1 + \\frac{0.06}{12})^{12} - 1 \\]\n\\[ \\text{EAR} = (1.005)^{12} - 1 \\]\nAs calculated earlier, (1.005)^12 ≈ 1.0616778\nThus, EAR ≈ 1.0616778 - 1 = 0.0616778 or 6.16778%\n\nSo, that is approximately 6.17% when rounded to the nearest hundredth.\n\nTherefore, the equivalent annual rate r is 6.17%.\n\nBut wait, in my initial step-by-step calculation, I thought 1.005^12 was about 1.06066, but that was incorrect because I must have miscalculated. The correct value is approximately 1.06167, leading to 6.17%.\n\nTherefore, the final answer is 6.17%, which is \\boxed{6.17}.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.17}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) that would give the same return if it were compounded annually instead of monthly, we need to calculate the effective annual rate (EAR) for a nominal annual interest rate of 6% compounded monthly.\n\nThe formula for the effective annual rate is:\n\\[ \\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n - 1 \\]\nwhere:\n- \\( r \\) is the nominal annual interest rate (6% or 0.06)\n- \\( n \\) is the number of compounding periods per year (12 for monthly)\n\nSubstituting the values:\n\\[ \\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} - 1 \\]\n\\[ \\text{EAR} = (1 + 0.005)^{12} - 1 \\]\n\\[ \\text{EAR} = (1.005)^{12} - 1 \\]\n\nCalculating \\( (1.005)^{12} \\):\nUsing logarithms or a step-by-step multiplication approach, we find:\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} \\approx 1.0616778 \\]\n\nThus:\n\\[ \\text{EAR} = 1.0616778 - 1 = 0.0616778 \\]\nConverting this to a percentage and rounding to the nearest hundredth:\n\\[ r \\approx 6.17\\% \\]\n\nTherefore, the equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.17}.",
