      "full_cot": "So, plugging in r = 0.06 and n = 19: 6.07} Wait, but in the computation above, I got 1.06066, which is 6.066%. So, rounding to the nearest hundredth is two decimal places, so 6.07%. Yes, that seems correct.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.07}.\n</think>\n\nDr. Fu Manchu has a bank account with an annual interest rate of 6 percent, compounded monthly. We need to find the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) that would give the same return if compounded annually.\n\nFirst, we use the compound interest formula for monthly compounding:\n\\[ A = P \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} \\]\n\nSince we are considering the amount after one year, we can set this equal to the amount after one year with annual compounding:\n\\[ P \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = P \\left(1 + r \\right) \\]\n\nThe principal \\( P \\) cancels out, so we have:\n\\[ \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\nCalculating the inside of the left side:\n\\[ 1 + \\frac{0.06}{12} = 1 + 0.005 = 1.005 \\]\n\nThus, the equation becomes:\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\nWe compute \\( (1.005)^{12} \\) step by step:\n\\[ 1.005^1 = 1.005 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^2 = 1.010025 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^3 \\approx 1.015075125 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^4 \\approx 1.020150501 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^5 \\approx 1.025251002 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^6 \\approx 1.030301502 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^7 \\approx 1.035355506 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^8 \\approx 1.040408016 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^9 \\approx 1.045461025 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^{10} \\approx 1.050525126 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^{11} \\approx 1.055600127 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^{12} \\approx 1.060660127 \\]\n\nTherefore, \\( (1.005)^{12} \\approx 1.06066 \\). This gives:\n\\[ 1 + r \\approx 1.06066 \\]\n\\[ r \\approx 0.06066 \\]\n\nRounding to the nearest hundredth, we get \\( r \\approx 0.0607 \\), which is 6.07%.\n\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.07}.",
