      "full_cot": "\\[ \\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + \\frac{0.6}{12}\\right)^{12} - 1 \\] 6.07}\n\nWait, no, actually, I think I confused myself. Let me correct that.\n\nWait, the formula is \\[ \\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n - 1 \\] So, in this case, r is 6%, which is 0.06, and n is 12. So, substituting in: \\[ \\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} - 1 \\] As before, 0.06 / 12 is 0.005, so: \\[ \\text{EAR} = (1.005)^{12} - 1 \\] Which we computed as approximately 1.06066 - 1 = 0.06066, which is 6.066%. So, rounded to the nearest hundredth, that's 6.07%. So, yeah, r is 6.07%. So, that seems to be the answer.\n\nWait, but let me make sure I didn't make any miscalculations. Let me check the exponentiation step again. Calculating (1.005)^12. Maybe using a different approach. Let's use the formula for compound interest again.\n\nAlternatively, I can use the natural logarithm and exponential function to calculate it. So, we have:\n\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} = e^{12 \\ln(1.005)} \\]\n\nFirst, compute ln(1.005). Let me approximate that. Since ln(1 + x) ≈ x - x^2/2 + x^3/3 - ..., for small x. So, x = 0.005. Therefore, ln(1.005) ≈ 0.005 - (0.005)^2 / 2 + (0.005)^3 / 3 - ...\n\nCalculating:\n\n0.005 = 0.005\n\n(0.005)^2 = 0.000025, so (0.005)^2 / 2 = 0.0000125\n\n(0.005)^3 = 0.000000125, so (0.005)^3 / 3 ≈ 0.000000041666...\n\nSo, ln(1.005) ≈ 0.005 - 0.0000125 + 0.000000041666 ≈ 0.004987541666\n\nSo, ln(1.005) ≈ 0.004987541666\n\nThen, 12 * ln(1.005) ≈ 12 * 0.004987541666 ≈ 0.0598479\n\nSo, \\( e^{0.0598479} \\) is approximately... Well, e^0.06 is approximately 1.06183654. So, 0.0598479 is just a bit less than 0.06, so the result will be slightly less than 1.06183654. Let me compute e^0.0598479.\n\nWe can use the Taylor series expansion for e^x around x = 0.06:\n\ne^x ≈ e^{0.06} + (x - 0.06)e^{0.06} + (x - 0.06)^2 / 2! * e^{0.06} + ...\n\nBut maybe it's too complicated. Alternatively, since 0.0598479 is approximately 0.06 - 0.0001521.\n\nSo, e^{0.0598479}