      "full_cot": "Wait, but just to make sure, maybe I should use a calculator to compute (1.015)^12 more accurately. 6.07}.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.07}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) that would give the same return as a bank account compounded monthly at an annual interest rate of 6 percent, we need to calculate the effective annual rate (EAR).\n\nThe formula for compound interest is:\n\\[ A = P \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n \\]\n\nFor monthly compounding, \\( r = 0.06 \\) and \\( n = 12 \\). We need to find the equivalent annual rate \\( r \\) such that:\n\\[ \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\nFirst, compute the inside of the left side:\n\\[ 1 + \\frac{0.06}{12} = 1 + 0.005 = 1.005 \\]\n\nNext, raise 1.005 to the 12th power:\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} \\approx 1.06066 \\]\n\nThis gives:\n\\[ 1.06066 = 1 + r \\]\n\nSolving for \\( r \\):\n\\[ r = 1.06066 - 1 = 0.06066 \\]\n\nConverting this to a percentage and rounding to the nearest hundredth:\n\\[ r \\approx 0.0607 \\]\n\\[ r \\approx 6.07\\% \\]\n\nTherefore, the equivalent annual interest rate is \\(\\boxed{6.07}\\).",
