    {
      "full_cot": "Alternatively, I can't use the formula for compound interest and calculate it step by step. 6.07\\%}.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.07\\%}.\n\\boxed{6.07}\n</think>\n\nTo find the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) percent for a bank account that compounds monthly at an annual interest rate of 6 percent, we need to calculate the effective annual rate (EAR).\n\nThe compound interest formula for monthly compounding is:\n\\[ A = P \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n \\]\nwhere \\( r \\) is the annual interest rate (6% or 0.06) and \\( n \\) is the number of compounding periods per year (12 for monthly).\n\nWe set the amount after one year with monthly compounding equal to the amount after one year with annual compounding:\n\\[ P \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = P \\left(1 + r \\right) \\]\n\nThe principal \\( P \\) cancels out, leaving:\n\\[ \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\nCalculating the inside of the left side:\n\\[ 1 + \\frac{0.06}{12} = 1 + 0.005 = 1.005 \\]\n\nSo, the equation becomes:\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\nUsing a calculator or step-by-step multiplication, we find:\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} \\approx 1.06066 \\]\n\nThus:\n\\[ 1 + r \\approx 1.06066 \\]\n\\[ r \\approx 0.06066 \\]\n\nRounding to the nearest hundredth, we get:\n\\[ r \\approx 0.0607 \\]\n\nTherefore, the equivalent annual interest rate is:\n\\[ \\boxed{6.07\\%} \\]",
