    {
      "full_cot": "Let me try computing 1.005 added to 12. 6.07\\%}.\n\nWait, but the question says \"annual interest rate of 6 percent, but it compounds monthly. If this is equivalent to a bank account that compounds annually at a rate of r percent, then what is r?\" So, they want the effective annual rate, which is approximately 6.07%.\n\nAlternatively, if I use the formula:\n\nEAR = (1 + (r/n))^n - 1\n\nSo, r is 6%, n is 12. So,\n\nEAR = (1 + 0.06/12)^12 - 1 = (1.005)^12 - 1 ≈ 1.06066017 - 1 = 0.06066017, so 6.066017%, which is approximately 6.07% when rounded to the nearest hundredth.\n\nHence, the answer is 6.07%.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.07}.\n\\boxed{6.07}\n</think>\n\nTo find the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) for a bank account that compounds monthly at an annual interest rate of 6 percent, we need to calculate the effective annual rate (EAR). The formula for the EAR is:\n\n\\[ \\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n - 1 \\]\n\nwhere \\( r \\) is the annual nominal interest rate (6% or 0.06) and \\( n \\) is the number of compounding periods per year (12 for monthly compounding).\n\n1. Calculate the monthly interest rate:\n   \\[ \\frac{0.06}{12} = 0.005 \\]\n\n2. Compute the compound factor for one year:\n   \\[ (1 + 0.005)^{12} \\approx 1.06066017 \\]\n\n3. Subtract 1 to find the EAR:\n   \\[ \\text{EAR} = 1.06066017 - 1 = 0.06066017 \\]\n\n4. Convert the EAR to a percentage and round to the nearest hundredth:\n   \\[ 0.06066017 \\approx 0.0607 \\]\n   \\[ r \\approx 6.07\\% \\]\n\nThus, the equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.07}.",
