l interest rate is \\boxed{6.17\\%}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) that would give the same return as a 6% annual interest rate compounded monthly, we need to calculate the effective annual rate (EAR).\n\nThe formula for the effective annual rate is:\n\\[ \\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n - 1 \\]\n\nGiven:\n- The annual interest rate \\( r = 6\\% = 0.06 \\)\n- The number of compounding periods per year \\( n = 12 \\)\n\nFirst, we compute the growth factor for monthly compounding:\n\\[ 1 + \\frac{0.06}{12} = 1 + 0.005 = 1.005 \\]\n\nNext, we raise this factor to the power of 12:\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} \\approx 1.061678 \\]\n\nThe effective annual rate is then:\n\\[ \\text{EAR} = 1.061678 - 1 = 0.061678 \\]\n\nConverting this to a percentage and rounding to the nearest hundredth:\n\\[ r \\approx 6.17\\% \\]\n\nThus, the equivalent annual interest rate is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6.17\\%}\n\\]",
