      "full_cot": "1.005^3 = 1.010025 * 1.105 = 1.015075125 } Wait, actually, in the problem statement, it's already given that the bank account has an annual interest rate of 6%, but it compounds monthly. So, the equivalent rate that would give the same return if compounded annually is the effective annual rate, which is approximately 6.07%. Therefore, r is approximately 6.07%.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.07}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) that would give the same return as a bank account with an annual interest rate of 6 percent compounded monthly, we need to calculate the effective annual rate (EAR).\n\nThe compound interest formula for one year is:\n\\[ A = P \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n \\]\n\nFor the monthly compounding, the rate per month is \\( \\frac{0.06}{12} = 0.005 \\). The amount after one year with monthly compounding is:\n\\[ A_{\\text{monthly}} = P \\left(1 + 0.005\\right)^{12} \\]\n\nFor the annual compounding, the amount after one year is:\n\\[ A_{\\text{annual}} = P (1 + r) \\]\n\nSince both amounts are equal, we set them equal to each other:\n\\[ P \\left(1 + 0.005\\right)^{12} = P (1 + r) \\]\n\nThe principal \\( P \\) cancels out, leaving:\n\\[ \\left(1 + 0.005\\right)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\nCalculating \\( \\left(1 + 0.005\\right)^{12} \\):\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} \\approx 1.06066 \\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\\[ 1.06066 = 1 + r \\]\n\nSolving for \\( r \\):\n\\[ r = 1.06066 - 1 = 0.06066 \\]\n\nConverting this to a percentage and rounding to the nearest hundredth:\n\\[ r \\approx 6.07\\% \\]\n\nTherefore, the equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.07}.",
