      "full_cot": "1.005^6 = 1.025251002 * 1.005 ≈ 7.030301502 6.07}\n\nWait, hold on, in the step-by-step multiplication above, I got 1.005^12 ≈ 1.060660127, so the EAR is approximately 6.066%, which is 6.07% when rounded to the nearest hundredth. So, that's correct.\n\nJust to recap, because I initially confused myself a bit, but now I'm confident. The monthly compounding increases the effective rate because you earn interest on the interest. So, the effective annual rate is higher than 6%. So, 6.07% is the equivalent annual rate. So, yeah, 6.07%.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.07}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) for a bank account that compounds monthly at an annual interest rate of 6 percent, we need to calculate the effective annual rate (EAR). \n\nThe formula for the effective annual rate is:\n\\[ \\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n - 1 \\]\nwhere \\( r \\) is the nominal annual interest rate and \\( n \\) is the number of times interest is compounded per year.\n\nGiven:\n- The nominal annual interest rate \\( r = 6\\% = 0.06 \\)\n- The number of compounding periods per year \\( n = 12 \\) (since it compounds monthly)\n\nFirst, we compute the factor inside the formula:\n\\[ 1 + \\frac{0.06}{12} = 1 + 0.005 = 1.005 \\]\n\nNext, we raise this factor to the power of 12:\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} \\approx 1.060660127 \\]\n\nThis result is then subtracted by 1 to find the EAR:\n\\[ \\text{EAR} = 1.060660127 - 1 = 0.060660127 \\]\n\nConverting this to a percentage and rounding to the nearest hundredth:\n\\[ r \\approx 0.0607 \\]\n\\[ r \\approx 6.07\\% \\]\n\nThus, the equivalent annual interest rate is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6.07}\n\\]",
