nearest hundredth.\n\nSo, that must be the correct answer.\n\nWait, let me double-check with another method.\n\nI can use the formula for the effective annual rate:\n\nEAR = (1 + (r/n))^n - 1\n\nWhere r = 0.06, n = 12.\n\nSo, plug in:\n\nEAR = (1 + 0.06/12)^12 - 1 = (1.005)^12 - 1 ≈ 1.0616778 - 1 = 0.0616778, so 6.16778%, which is 6.17% when rounded to the nearest hundredth.\n\nTherefore, the equivalent annual rate r is approximately 6.17%.\n\nWait, so earlier I thought it was 6.07%, but that was a mistake. The correct calculation leads to 6.17%.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check using logarithms. Let me compute ln(1.0616778) to see if it equals 0.059748.\n\nCompute ln(1.0616778):\n\nUsing the Taylor series for ln(1+x) around x=0:\n\nln(1+x) = x - x^2/2 + x^3/3 - x^4/4 + ...\n\nLet x = 0.0616778.\n\nCompute ln(1.0616778):\n\nln(1.0616778) = 0.0616778 - (0.0616778)^2 / 2 + (0.0616778)^3 / 3 - (0.0616778)^4 / 4 + ...\n\nCompute each term:\n\nFirst term: 0.0616778\n\nSecond term: (0.0616778)^2 / 2 ≈ 0.003815 / 2 ≈ 0.0019075\n\nThird term: (0.0616778)^3 / 3 ≈ 0.0002352 / 3 ≈ 0.0000784\n\nFourth term: (0.0616778)^4 / 4 ≈ 0.00001453 / 4 ≈ 0.0000036325\n\nSo, ln(1.0616778) ≈ 0.0616778 - 0.0019075 + 0.0000784 - 0.0000036325 ≈\n\n0.0616778 - 0.0019075 = 0.0597703\n\n0.0597703 + 0.0000784 = 0.0598487\n\n0.0598487 - 0.0000036325 ≈ 0.059845\n\nSo, ln(1.0616778) ≈ 0.059845, which is very close to the 0.059748 we had earlier, the slight difference is due to the approximation in the Taylor series.\n\nTherefore, exponentiating e^0.059845 ≈ 1.0616778, which is consistent.\n\nTherefore, (1.005)^12 ≈ 1.0616778, so r ≈ 0.0616778, which is 6.16778%, so 6.17% when rounded to the nearest hundredth.\n\nTherefore, the equivalent annual interest rate is 6.17%.\n\nI think earlier I mistakenly thought that (1.005)^12 was approximately 1.06066, but that must have been a miscalculation. Upon more careful step-by-step multiplication, it's approximately 1.06168.\n\nSo, to sum up, the effective annual rate for a 6% nominal rate compounded monthly is approximately 6.17%.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.17}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) that would give the same return as a bank account with a 6% annual interest rate compounded monthly, we need to calculate the effective annual rate (EAR).\n\nThe compound interest formula for monthly compounding is:\n\\[ A = P \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n \\]\nwhere \\( r = 0.06 \\) and \\( n = 12 \\).\n\nSince we are comparing the amount after one year, we can set the expressions for the amount equal:\n\\[ P \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = P \\left(1 + r \\right) \\]\n\nThe principal \\( P \\) cancels out, leaving:\n\\[ \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\nCalculating the inside of the left side:\n\\[ 1 + \\frac{0.06}{12} = 1 + 0.005 = 1.005 \\]\n\nThus, the equation becomes:\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\nWe need to compute \\( (1.005)^{12} \\). Using step-by-step multiplication, we find:\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} \\approx 1.0616778 \\]\n\nTherefore:\n\\[ 1 + r = 1.0616778 \\]\n\\[ r = 1.0616778 - 1 = 0.0616778 \\]\n\nConverting this to a percentage and rounding to the nearest hundredth:\n\\[ r \\approx 6.17\\% \\]\n\nThus, the equivalent annual interest rate is:\n\\[ \\boxed{6.17} \\]",
