    {
      "full_cot": "x = 0.05, so ln(1.005) ≈ 0.005 - (0.005)^2 / 2 + (0.005)^3 / 3 - (0.005)^4 / 4 06.07} Wait, no, LaTeX notation is \\boxed{6.07}.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.07}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) for a bank account that compounds monthly at an annual interest rate of 6 percent, we need to calculate the effective annual rate (EAR). The formula for the effective annual rate is:\n\n\\[ \\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n - 1 \\]\n\nwhere \\( r \\) is the annual interest rate (in decimal) and \\( n \\) is the number of times interest is compounded per year. For this problem, \\( r = 0.06 \\) and \\( n = 12 \\).\n\nFirst, we compute the monthly interest rate:\n\n\\[ \\frac{0.06}{12} = 0.005 \\]\n\nNext, we calculate the effective annual rate by raising \\( 1 + 0.005 \\) to the 12th power:\n\n\\[ (1 + 0.005)^{12} \\]\n\nUsing a step-by-step calculation:\n\n\\[ 1.005^{12} \\approx 1.060660127 \\]\n\nSubtracting 1 from this result gives us the effective annual rate:\n\n\\[ 1.060660127 - 1 = 0.060660127 \\]\n\nConverting this to a percentage and rounding to the nearest hundredth, we get:\n\n\\[ 0.06066 \\approx 0.0607 \\]\n\nThus, the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{6.07} \\]",
      "sentence_count": 21,
      "token_count": 346,
      "uncertainty_word_count": 1,
      "uncertainty_occurrences": [
