    {
      "full_cot": "Compute the last term: } Wait, no, actually, in the problem statement, the user wrote: \"Problem: Dr. Fu Manchu has a bank account that has an annual interest rate of 6 percent, but it compounds monthly. If this is equivalent to a bank account that compounds annually at a rate of r percent, then what is r? (Give your answer to the nearest hundredth.)\" So, the question is asking for the rate r, which is the EAR, so approximately 6.07%. So, I think that's solid. So, to recap, the steps were:\n\n1. Recognize that the effective annual rate (EAR) is needed when comparing a monthly compounding rate to an annual compounding rate.\n\n2. Use the formula for EAR: \\( \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n - 1 \\), where r is the nominal rate and n is the number of compounding periods per year.\n\n3. Substitute r = 0.06 and n = 12 into the formula.\n\n4. Calculate \\( (1 + 0.06/12)^{12} \\approx 1.06066 \\).\n\n5. Subtract 1 to get the EAR rate, which is approximately 0.06066 or 6.066%.\n\n6. Round to the nearest hundredth, which gives 6.07%.\n\nTherefore, the equivalent annual interest rate is 6.07%.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.07}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) that would give the same return if compounded annually instead of monthly, we need to calculate the effective annual rate (EAR). \n\nThe compound interest formula for monthly compounding is:\n\\[ A = P \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n \\]\nwhere \\( r = 0.06 \\) and \\( n = 12 \\).\n\nSince we are looking for the equivalent annual rate, we set the amount after one year with monthly compounding equal to the amount after one year with annual compounding:\n\\[ P \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = P \\left(1 + r \\right) \\]\n\nThe principal \\( P \\) cancels out, leaving:\n\\[ \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\nCalculating the inside of the left side:\n\\[ 1 + \\frac{0.06}{12} = 1 + 0.005 = 1.005 \\]\n\nSo, we need to compute:\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} \\]\n\nUsing step-by-step multiplication:\n\\[ 1.005^1 = 1.005 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^2 = 1.010025 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^3 \\approx 1.015075125 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^4 \\approx 1.020150501 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^5 \\approx 1.025251002 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^6 \\approx 1.030301502 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^7 \\approx 1.035355506 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^8 \\approx 1.040408016 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^9 \\approx 1.045461025 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^{10} \\approx 1.050525126 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^{11} \\approx 1.055600127 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^{12} \\approx 1.060660127 \\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} \\approx 1.06066 \\]\n\nSo, the effective annual rate \\( r \\) is:\n\\[ r = 1.06066 - 1 = 0.06066 \\]\n\nRounding to the nearest hundredth, we get:\n\\[ r \\approx 0.0607 \\]\n\nTherefore, the equivalent annual interest rate is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6.07}\n\\]",
