tep-by-step, I must have miscalculated somewhere, getting a lower value.\n\nTherefore, using the accurate step-by-step, I get 1.061678, so \\( r ≈ 6.17\\% \\).\n\nWait, but in the logarithm method, I also got approximately 6.167%, so that's consistent.\n\nSo, the error in my first step-by-step was that I might have stopped too early or miscalculated one of the exponents.\n\nTherefore, the correct effective annual rate is approximately 6.17%.\n\nBut let me check with another approach. The formula for effective annual rate is:\n\n\\( \\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n - 1 \\)\n\nWhere r is the nominal annual rate, and n is the number of compounding periods per year.\n\nSo, plugging in r = 0.06 and n = 12:\n\n\\( \\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} - 1 = (1.005)^{12} - 1 ≈ 1.061678 - 1 = 0.061678 \\)\n\nSo, 0.061678, which is 6.1678%, so 6.17% when rounded to the nearest hundredth.\n\nTherefore, the equivalent annual interest rate is 6.17%.\n\nSo, the initial mistake was in my first step-by-step calculation where I incorrectly thought it was 6.066%, which was wrong.\n\nTherefore, after correcting, I get 6.17%.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.17\\%}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) for a bank account that compounds monthly at an annual interest rate of 6 percent, we need to calculate the effective annual rate (EAR). The formula for the effective annual rate is:\n\n\\[ \\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n - 1 \\]\n\nwhere \\( r \\) is the nominal annual interest rate and \\( n \\) is the number of compounding periods per year. In this case, \\( r = 0.06 \\) and \\( n = 12 \\).\n\nFirst, we compute the factor inside the parentheses:\n\n\\[ 1 + \\frac{0.06}{12} = 1 + 0.005 = 1.005 \\]\n\nNext, we raise this factor to the power of 12:\n\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} \\]\n\nUsing a step-by-step multiplication approach or a calculator, we find:\n\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} \\approx 1.061678 \\]\n\nSubtracting 1 from this result gives us the effective annual rate:\n\n\\[ \\text{EAR} = 1.061678 - 1 = 0.061678 \\]\n\nConverting this to a percentage and rounding to the nearest hundredth, we get:\n\n\\[ r \\approx 6.17\\% \\]\n\nThus, the equivalent annual interest rate is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6.17\\%}\n\\]",
