      "full_cot": "So, e^{0.59849692} ≈ ? 6.07}.\n\nWait, I think I just got confused. No, actually, in the previous steps, we had \\( (1.005)^{12} ≈ 1.06066 \\), so that's 6.066%, which is approximately 6.07%. So, yeah, that's correct. So, the equivalent annual rate is 6.07%. Therefore, r is 6.07%.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.07}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) that would give the same return as a 6% annual interest rate compounded monthly, we need to calculate the effective annual rate (EAR).\n\nThe compound interest formula is:\n\\[ A = P \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n \\]\n\nFor the monthly compounding, the rate per period is:\n\\[ \\frac{0.06}{12} = 0.005 \\]\n\nThe amount after one year with monthly compounding is:\n\\[ A_{\\text{monthly}} = P \\left(1 + 0.005\\right)^{12} \\]\n\nFor the annual compounding, the amount after one year is:\n\\[ A_{\\text{annual}} = P (1 + r) \\]\n\nSetting these equal gives:\n\\[ P \\left(1 + 0.005\\right)^{12} = P (1 + r) \\]\n\nCanceling \\( P \\) from both sides:\n\\[ \\left(1 + 0.005\\right)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\nCalculating \\( \\left(1.005\\right)^{12} \\):\n\\[ \\left(1.005\\right)^{12} \\approx 1.06066 \\]\n\nThus:\n\\[ 1 + r = 1.06066 \\]\n\\[ r = 1.06066 - 1 \\]\n\\[ r = 0.06066 \\]\n\nConverting this to a percentage and rounding to the nearest hundredth:\n\\[ r \\approx 6.07\\% \\]\n\nTherefore, the equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.07}.",
