      "full_cot": "So, let me compute e^{0.051849692}. 6.07}.\n\nWait, hold on, when I computed (1.005)^12 step by step, I got approximately 1.06066, which is about 6.066%, which rounds to 6.07%. But when I tried to compute it with logarithms, I got a number very close to e^{0.059849692}, which is approximately 1.0607 as well. So, both methods give the same result. Therefore, I can be confident that the effective annual rate is approximately 6.07%. Therefore, r is 6.07% when rounded to the nearest hundredth.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.07}.\n\\boxed{6.07}\n</think>\n\nTo find the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) that would give the same return as a 6% annual interest rate compounded monthly, we need to calculate the effective annual rate (EAR).\n\nThe formula for compound interest is:\n\\[ A = P \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n \\]\n\nFor monthly compounding:\n\\[ A_{\\text{monthly}} = P \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} \\]\n\nFor annual compounding:\n\\[ A_{\\text{annual}} = P (1 + r) \\]\n\nSince the amounts after one year should be equal:\n\\[ P \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = P (1 + r) \\]\n\nThe principal \\( P \\) cancels out:\n\\[ \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\nSimplifying inside the parentheses:\n\\[ 1 + \\frac{0.06}{12} = 1 + 0.005 = 1.005 \\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\nCalculating \\( (1.005)^{12} \\) step by step:\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} \\approx 1.060660127 \\]\n\nTherefore:\n\\[ 1 + r = 1.060660127 \\]\n\\[ r = 1.060660127 - 1 \\]\n\\[ r \\approx 0.06066 \\]\n\nRounding to the nearest hundredth:\n\\[ r \\approx 0.0607 \\]\n\nConverting to a percentage:\n\\[ r \\approx 6.07\\% \\]\n\nThus, the equivalent annual interest rate is \\(\\boxed{6.07}\\).",
