      "full_cot": "We can use the Taylor series expansion around 0.07: 6.07\\%}\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.07\\%}.\n</think>\n\nDr. Fu Manchu has a bank account with an annual interest rate of 6 percent, compounded monthly. We need to find the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) percent that would give the same return if it were compounded annually.\n\nFirst, we use the compound interest formula to find the effective annual rate (EAR). The compound interest formula is:\n\\[ A = P \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n \\]\n\nFor monthly compounding, the nominal rate \\( r \\) is 6% or 0.06, and the number of compounding periods \\( n \\) is 12. We set the amount after one year with monthly compounding equal to the amount after one year with annual compounding:\n\\[ P \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = P \\left(1 + r \\right) \\]\n\nThe principal \\( P \\) cancels out, leaving:\n\\[ \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\nCalculating the inside of the left side:\n\\[ 1 + \\frac{0.06}{12} = 1 + 0.005 = 1.005 \\]\n\nThus, the equation becomes:\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\nWe compute \\( (1.005)^{12} \\) step by step:\n\\[ 1.005^1 = 1.005 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^2 = 1.010025 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^3 \\approx 1.015075125 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^4 \\approx 1.020150501 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^5 \\approx 1.025251002 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^6 \\approx 1.030301502 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^7 \\approx 1.035355506 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^8 \\approx 1.040408016 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^9 \\approx 1.045461025 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^{10} \\approx 1.050525126 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^{11} \\approx 1.055600127 \\]\n\\[ 1.005^{12} \\approx 1.060660127 \\]\n\nThus, \\( (1.005)^{12} \\approx 1.06066 \\). Therefore:\n\\[ 1 + r = 1.06066 \\]\n\\[ r = 1.06066 - 1 = 0.06066 \\]\n\nRounded to the nearest hundredth, \\( r \\) is 6.07%.\n\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.07\\%}.",
