    {
      "full_cot": "So, e^{-Δx} ≈ 1 + Δx + (Δx)^2 / 2 6.07\\%}\n\nWait, but I think I went too far into the calculation, but in any case, both methods give me approximately 6.06%, which is about 6.07% when rounded to the nearest hundredth. So, I think that's solid.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.07\\%}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) percent for a bank account that compounds monthly at an annual interest rate of 6 percent, we need to calculate the effective annual rate (EAR). The formula for the effective annual rate is:\n\n\\[ \\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n - 1 \\]\n\nwhere \\( r \\) is the annual interest rate (in decimal form) and \\( n \\) is the number of times interest is compounded per year. In this case, \\( r = 0.06 \\) and \\( n = 12 \\).\n\nFirst, we compute the inside of the formula:\n\n\\[ 1 + \\frac{0.06}{12} = 1 + 0.005 = 1.005 \\]\n\nNext, we raise this value to the 12th power:\n\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} \\]\n\nUsing the compound interest formula, we find:\n\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} \\approx 1.060660127 \\]\n\nSubtracting 1 from this result gives us the effective annual rate:\n\n\\[ 1.060660127 - 1 = 0.060660127 \\]\n\nConverting this back to a percentage and rounding to the nearest hundredth, we get:\n\n\\[ 0.06066 \\approx 0.0607 \\]\n\nThus, the equivalent annual interest rate is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6.07\\%}\n\\]",
