      "full_cot": "≈ 1 - 0.000150308 + (0.100150308)^2 / 2 6.07}.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{6.07}\n</think>\n\nTo find the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) that would give the same return if compounded annually, we need to calculate the effective annual rate (EAR) for the given monthly compounding rate. \n\nThe formula for compound interest is:\n\\[ A = P \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n \\]\n\nGiven:\n- The annual interest rate is 6%, or \\( r = 0.06 \\).\n- The interest is compounded monthly, so \\( n = 12 \\).\n\nWe set the amount after one year with monthly compounding equal to the amount after one year with annual compounding:\n\\[ P \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = P \\left(1 + r \\right) \\]\n\nThe principal \\( P \\) cancels out, so we have:\n\\[ \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\nCalculating the inside of the left side:\n\\[ 1 + \\frac{0.06}{12} = 1 + 0.005 = 1.005 \\]\n\nThus, the equation becomes:\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\nWe need to compute \\( (1.005)^{12} \\):\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} \\approx 1.06066 \\]\n\nSo,\n\\[ 1 + r \\approx 1.06066 \\]\n\\[ r \\approx 0.06066 \\]\n\nRounding to the nearest hundredth, we get:\n\\[ r \\approx 0.0607 \\]\n\\[ r \\approx 6.07\\% \\]\n\nTherefore, the equivalent annual interest rate is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6.07}\n\\]",
