      "full_cot": "≈ 0.799849703 } 6.07\n\nWait, actually, I think I might have made a miscalculation in the last step. Let me correct that. So, if e^{0.059849692} = e^{0.06 - 0.000150308} ≈ e^{0.06} * e^{-0.000150308}. We know e^{0.06} ≈ 1.06183654, and e^{-0.000150308} ≈ 1 - 0.000150308 + (0.000150308)^2 / 2 ≈ 1 - 0.000150308 + 0.00000001118 ≈ 0.99984969. So, multiplying them: 1.06183654 * 0.99984969 ≈ 1.06183654 - (1.06183654 * 0.000150308) ≈ 1.06183654 - 0.00015935 ≈ 1.06167719. So, that's approximately 1.06167719. Wait, but earlier step-by-step multiplication gave me 1.060660127, which is a bit different. Hmm, so which one is more accurate?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to use the formula for EAR directly: \\[ \\text{EAR} = (1 + \\frac{r}{n})^n - 1 \\] So, r is 0.06, n is 12, so: \\[ \\text{EAR} = (1 + 0.06/12)^{12} - 1 = (1.005)^{12} - 1 \\] Which is approximately 0.060660127 as we calculated before. So, 6.066%, which is approximately 6.07% when rounded to the nearest hundredth. Therefore, the equivalent annual rate is 6.07%. So, I think that's the answer. Let me just recap: monthly compounding at 6% leads to an effective annual rate of approximately 6.07%, so the equivalent annual rate is 6.07%. Thus, r is 6.07%.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.07}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) that would give the same return if it were compounded annually instead of monthly, we need to calculate the effective annual rate (EAR) for the given monthly compounding rate.\n\nThe compound interest formula is:\n\\[ A = P \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n \\]\nwhere \\( r \\) is the annual interest rate (in decimal) and \\( n \\) is the number of times interest is compounded per year.\n\nFor monthly compounding, \\( r = 0.06 \\) and \\( n = 12 \\). We set the amount after one year with monthly compounding equal to the amount after one year with annual compounding:\n\\[ P \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = P (1 + r) \\]\n\nThe principal \\( P \\) cancels out, so we have:\n\\[ \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\nCalculating the inside of the left side:\n\\[ 1 + \\frac{0.06}{12} = 1 + 0.005 = 1.005 \\]\n\nThus, the equation becomes:\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\nWe compute \\( (1.005)^{12} \\) step by step:\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} \\approx 1.060660127 \\]\n\nTherefore:\n\\[ 1 + r \\approx 1.060660127 \\]\n\\[ r \\approx 1.060660127 - 1 \\]\n\\[ r \\approx 0.060660127 \\]\n\nConverting this to a percentage and rounding to the nearest hundredth:\n\\[ r \\approx 6.07\\% \\]\n\nThus, the equivalent annual interest rate is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6.07}\n\\]",
