      "full_cot": "So, 1.06183654 - 0.00015959 ≈ 2.06167695 6.07\\%}\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.07\\%}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) percent that would give the same return as a bank account that compounds monthly at an annual interest rate of 6 percent, we need to calculate the effective annual rate (EAR).\n\nFirst, we use the compound interest formula for monthly compounding:\n\\[ A = P \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n \\]\nwhere \\( r = 0.06 \\) and \\( n = 12 \\). For one year, the amount \\( A \\) should be the same whether compounded monthly or annually. Therefore, we set the expressions for \\( A \\) equal:\n\\[ P \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = P (1 + r) \\]\n\nThe principal \\( P \\) cancels out, leaving:\n\\[ \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\nSimplifying the inside of the left side:\n\\[ 1 + \\frac{0.06}{12} = 1 + 0.005 = 1.005 \\]\n\nThus, the equation becomes:\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\nWe need to compute \\( (1.005)^{12} \\). Using step-by-step multiplication:\n\\[ 1.005^{12} \\approx 1.060660127 \\]\n\nTherefore:\n\\[ 1.060660127 = 1 + r \\]\n\\[ r = 1.060660127 - 1 \\]\n\\[ r \\approx 0.060660127 \\]\n\nConverting this to a percentage and rounding to the nearest hundredth:\n\\[ r \\approx 6.07\\% \\]\n\nThus, the equivalent annual interest rate is \\(\\boxed{6.07\\%}\\).",
