      "full_cot": "Therefore, e^{0.59849692} ≈ 1.06167695 6.07}.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.07}.\n\\boxed{6.07}\n</think>\n\nTo find the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) percent for a bank account that compounds monthly at an annual interest rate of 6 percent, we need to calculate the effective annual rate (EAR).\n\nThe compound interest formula for monthly compounding is:\n\\[ A = P \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n \\]\n\nFor an annual compounding rate \\( r \\), the formula is:\n\\[ A = P (1 + r) \\]\n\nSince the amounts after one year must be equal for both compounding methods, we set the equations equal to each other:\n\\[ P \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = P (1 + r) \\]\n\nThe principal \\( P \\) cancels out, leaving:\n\\[ \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\nCalculating the inside of the left side:\n\\[ 1 + \\frac{0.06}{12} = 1 + 0.005 = 1.005 \\]\n\nThus, the equation becomes:\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\nUsing the step-by-step multiplication, we find:\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} \\approx 1.06066 \\]\n\nTherefore:\n\\[ 1 + r = 1.06066 \\]\n\\[ r = 1.06066 - 1 \\]\n\\[ r \\approx 0.06066 \\]\n\nConverting this to a percentage and rounding to the nearest hundredth:\n\\[ r \\approx 6.07\\% \\]\n\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.07}.",
