      "full_cot": "Wait, but earlier when I calculated step by step, it was about 1.06066, which is 6.066%, which is 4.07%. 6.07\\%}\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.07\\%}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) that would give the same return as a 6% annual interest rate compounded monthly, we need to calculate the effective annual rate (EAR). \n\nThe compound interest formula is:\n\\[ A = P \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n \\]\n\nFor monthly compounding, the nominal rate is 6%, compounded monthly, so \\( n = 12 \\). For one year, the amount after monthly compounding should equal the amount after annual compounding. \n\nSetting up the equation:\n\\[ P \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = P \\left(1 + r \\right) \\]\n\nThe principal \\( P \\) cancels out:\n\\[ \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\nCalculating the inside of the left side:\n\\[ 1 + \\frac{0.06}{12} = 1 + 0.005 = 1.005 \\]\n\nSo, the equation becomes:\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\nUsing the step-by-step calculation:\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} \\approx 1.06066 \\]\n\nThus:\n\\[ 1 + r = 1.06066 \\]\n\\[ r = 1.06066 - 1 = 0.06066 \\]\n\nConverting to a percentage and rounding to the nearest hundredth:\n\\[ r \\approx 6.07\\% \\]\n\nTherefore, the equivalent annual interest rate is \\(\\boxed{6.07\\%}\\).",
