      "full_cot": "1.005^5 ≈ 1.020150501 + 1.005 ≈ 1.025251002 6.07}\n\nWait, hold on. Let me use the exact value. If (1.005)^12 is approximately 1.0606601, then r = 1.0606601 - 1 = 0.0606601, which is 6.06601%. So, 6.06601% when rounded to the nearest hundredth is 6.07%. Therefore, the equivalent annual interest rate is 6.07%. So, I think the earlier step-by-step calculation is more accurate because it's precise, and the logarithm approach might have introduced some approximation errors. So, I think I can confidently say that r is approximately 6.07%.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.07}.\n\\boxed{6.07}\n</think>\n\nTo find the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) that would give the same return as a 6% annual interest rate compounded monthly, we need to calculate the effective annual rate (EAR).\n\nThe formula for compound interest is:\n\\[ A = P \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n \\]\n\nFor monthly compounding, the rate per period is:\n\\[ \\frac{0.06}{12} = 0.005 \\]\n\nThe amount after one year with monthly compounding is:\n\\[ P \\left(1 + 0.005\\right)^{12} \\]\n\nWe need this to be equal to the amount after one year with annual compounding:\n\\[ P (1 + r) \\]\n\nSetting these equal, we get:\n\\[ \\left(1 + 0.005\\right)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\nCalculating \\( (1.005)^{12} \\):\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} \\approx 1.060660127 \\]\n\nThus:\n\\[ 1.060660127 = 1 + r \\]\n\\[ r = 1.060660127 - 1 \\]\n\\[ r \\approx 0.060660127 \\]\n\nConverting this to a percentage and rounding to the nearest hundredth:\n\\[ r \\approx 6.07\\% \\]\n\nTherefore, the equivalent annual interest rate is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6.07}\n\\]",
