s wrong due to miscalculations or truncation. So, the accurate calculation shows that the effective annual rate is approximately 6.17%.\n\nBut wait, the question says to give the answer to the nearest hundredth. So, 6.17% is already to the nearest hundredth. Therefore, the answer is 6.17%.\n\nBut in my initial step-by-step, I thought it was 6.07%, which is wrong because I didn't carry enough decimal places. So, in conclusion, the correct equivalent annual interest rate is 6.17%.\n\nBut hold on, let me check a source or recall the formula for EAR again.\n\nThe formula is: EAR = (1 + (rate / number of periods))^(number of periods) - 1\n\nSo, with rate = 6%, periods = 12.\n\nSo, (1 + 0.06/12)^12 -1 = (1.005)^12 -1 ≈ 1.061678 -1 = 0.061678 or 6.1678%\n\nSo, 6.17% when rounded to the nearest hundredth.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 6.17%.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.17}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) that would give the same return as a bank account with an annual interest rate of 6 percent compounded monthly, we need to calculate the effective annual rate (EAR).\n\nThe compound interest formula is:\n\\[ A = P \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n \\]\n\nFor monthly compounding, \\( r = 0.06 \\) and \\( n = 12 \\). We need to find the equivalent annual rate \\( r \\) such that:\n\\[ P \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = P (1 + r) \\]\n\nSubstituting the values, we get:\n\\[ \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\\[ \\left(1 + 0.005\\right)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\nCalculating \\( (1.005)^{12} \\) step-by-step:\n1. \\( 1.005^1 = 1.005 \\)\n2.",
