r \\) that would give the same return as a bank account that compounds monthly at an annual interest rate of 6 percent, we need to calculate the effective annual rate (EAR).\n\nThe formula for the effective annual rate is:\n\\[ \\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n - 1 \\]\nwhere \\( r \\) is the annual interest rate (in decimal) and \\( n \\) is the number of times interest is compounded per year.\n\nGiven:\n- Annual interest rate, \\( r = 0.06 \\)\n- Compounded monthly, so \\( n = 12 \\)\n\nFirst, we compute the factor inside the formula:\n\\[ 1 + \\frac{0.06}{12} = 1 + 0.005 = 1.005 \\]\n\nNext, we raise this factor to the power of 12:\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} \\]\n\nUsing step-by-step multiplication or a calculator, we find:\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} \\approx 1.060660127 \\]\n\nSubtracting 1 from this result gives the EAR:\n\\[ \\text{EAR} = 1.060660127 - 1 = 0.060660127 \\]\n\nConverting this to a percentage and rounding to the nearest hundredth:\n\\[ 0.060660127 \\approx 0.0607 \\]\nThus, the effective annual rate is 6.07%.\n\nTherefore, the equivalent annual interest rate is:\n\\[ \\boxed{6.07\\%} \\]",
