    {
      "full_cot": "Therefore, the equivalent annual interest rate is 6.07%. 6.07\\%}\n\nWait, hold on. In the problem statement, it's said that the annual interest rate is 6%, but it compounds monthly. So, the nominal rate is 6%, but compounding monthly. So, to find the equivalent annual rate, which is r, it's the EAR, which is approximately 6.07%. So, the answer is 6.07%, which is 6.07 when rounded to the nearest hundredth. Therefore, I think that is correct.\n\nBut just to make sure, let me think about another way. The formula for EAR is: \\[ \\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + \\frac{\\text{nominal rate}}{\\text{number of compounding periods per year}}\\right)^{\\text{number of compounding periods per year}} - 1 \\] So, here, nominal rate is 6%, so 0.06, number of compounding periods per year is 12, since it's monthly. So, \\[ \\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} - 1 \\] Which is exactly the formula we used before, giving approximately 6.066%, so 6.07%. Therefore, I'm confident that 6.07% is the correct answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.07}.\n\\\n\nTo find the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) that would give the same return as an account compounding monthly at a 6% annual rate, we need to calculate the effective annual rate (EAR). The formula for EAR when compounding monthly is:\n\n\\[ \\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} - 1 \\]\n\nFirst, compute the inside of the left side:\n\n\\[ 1 + \\frac{0.06}{12} = 1 + 0.005 = 1.005 \\]\n\nNext, raise this to the 12th power:\n\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} \\approx 1.06066 \\]\n\nSubtract 1 to find the EAR:\n\n\\[ \\text{EAR} = 1.06066 - 1 = 0.06066 \\]\n\nConvert this to a percentage and round to the nearest hundredth:\n\n\\[ r = 0.06066 \\times 100 \\approx 6.066\\% \\]\n\nRounded to the nearest hundredth, \\( r \\) is approximately 6.07%.\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6.07}\n\\]",
